var e=JSON.parse(`[[2501,"Use useMemo and useCallback only when there is a measurable performance problem. They are useful for preventing expensive repaints or repeat computations when the dependent values do not change. Do not apply them by default — that can make the performance worse, because of the overhead of the memoization. Assess the complexity of the computation and the frequency of the re-renders.","When applying useMemo and useCallback is justified\\nThese React hooks are intended for optimizing the performance, but their use has to be warranted. The main rule: apply them only when you are observing real performance problems, such as frequent re-renders or expensive computations.\\n\\nThe criteria for using them\\n\\n- Expensive computations: if the function inside a useMemo performs complex operations (sorting a large array, filtering thousands of elements, for example), the memoization may be useful.\\n- Stable references: useCallback is needed when you pass a callback into a child component wrapped in React.memo and want to avoid its re-render when the dependencies are unchanged.\\n- A measurable benefit: use profiling tools (the React DevTools Profiler), in order to be sure the memoization really does cut the render time down.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen not to apply them\\n\\n- For simple computations (the addition of numbers, for example) — the overhead of the memoization exceeds the benefit.\\n- For callbacks that are passed into native HTML elements — they do not use React.memo.\\n- Without profiling — premature optimization can complicate the code without any real gain.\\n\\nConclusion: use useMemo and useCallback only after you have revealed performance bottlenecks by means of a profiler. They are useful for expensive computations and for stable references in components with React.memo, but they should not be applied by default.","import React, { useMemo, useCallback, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction ExpensiveList({ items, filter }) {\\n  // Expensive filtering — a useMemo is justified\\n  const filteredItems = useMemo(() => {\\n    return items.filter(item => item.name.includes(filter));\\n  }, [items, filter]);\\n\\n  // A simple computation — no useMemo is needed\\n  const count = items.length;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {filteredItems.map(item => (\\n        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  \\n  // A useCallback for a stable reference\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>{count}</button>\\n      <ExpensiveList items={bigArray} filter=\\"test\\" />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2502,"To cancel a setTimeout inside a hook, use the cleanup function in useEffect. Save the timer's identifier returned by setTimeout and call clearTimeout in the function you return. That guarantees the timer will be cancelled when the component unmounts or when the effect runs again.","The problem with setTimeout in React\\nWhen using setTimeout inside a React component, there is a risk that the callback will run after the component has already unmounted. That can lead to memory leaks, to errors about updating the state of an unmounted component, or to unexpected behaviour.\\n\\nThe solution by means of useEffect and a cleanup function\\nReact provides a clean-up mechanism through the return of a function from useEffect. That function is called when the component unmounts or before the effect runs again. Use it for calling clearTimeout.\\n\\nHandling the dependencies and the reset of the state\\nIf the timer has to restart when particular values change, state them in the array of dependencies. On every change of a dependency the effect restarts: first the cleanup is called (cancelling the old timer), then a new one is created.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAlways use a cleanup function in useEffect in order to cancel a setTimeout. That is an obligatory practice for preventing memory leaks and errors in React applications, especially when working with asynchronous operations and timers.","import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TimerComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const timerId = setTimeout(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    // The clean-up function\\n    return () => {\\n      clearTimeout(timerId);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies — it runs once\\n\\n  return <div>Count: {count}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const timerId = setTimeout(() => {\\n    // the action\\n  }, delay);\\n\\n  return () => clearTimeout(timerId);\\n}, [delay]); // a restart when delay changes"],[2503,"For a small one-off SPA project, adopting a strict architecture such as FSD is normally excessive. An architecture's main aim is to support scaling and long-term maintainability. If the project will not be developed further, a minimal organization of the code will do. Even in a small project, however, it is useful to observe the basic principles, such as the separation of responsibility, in order to avoid chaos.","What is an architecture for?\\nA project's architecture is a set of conventions and structural decisions, which help you organize the code so that it is easy to understand, to change and to extend. Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is one of the modern approaches for frontend projects, which offers a clear separation by functional module (by layer).\\n\\nWhen is an architecture obligatory?\\nIf the project is planned to be developed over a long time, if several developers work on it, or if it will be integrated with other systems — an architecture is necessary. It reduces the technical debt and speeds the addition of new functionality up.\\n\\nWhen can you do without a strict architecture?\\nFor a one-off SPA project that will not be maintained after delivery, a strict architecture such as FSD can be superfluous. In that case it is enough to:\\n- Separate the code into logical blocks (components, utilities, API requests, for example).\\n- Avoid global variables and strong coupling.\\n- Use a simple folder structure, such as \`components/\`, \`utils/\`, \`pages/\`.\\n\\nAn example of a minimal structure for a small SPA\\n\\nSuch an approach requires no adoption of FSD but preserves the readability and lets you find the file you need quickly.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor a one-off small SPA project, adopting FSD is excessive. A basic organization of the code with a separation by folder is enough. If the project will live longer or grow, it is worth considering a stricter architecture, in order to avoid problems with maintenance.","src/\\n  components/\\n    Header.js\\n    Footer.js\\n  pages/\\n    Home.js\\n    About.js\\n  utils/\\n    api.js\\n    helpers.js\\n  App.js\\n  index.js"],[2504,"For an SPA with authorization and one multi-step form, a state manager is not obligatory. You can use the components' local state and React context for passing the data between the form's steps. A state manager is justified for a complex interaction of a great many components, for caching data or for synchronizing with a server. In this case it would add excessive complexity without any substantial benefit.","When is a state manager necessary?\\nA state manager (Redux, MobX, Zustand, for example) solves the problem of managing global state in large applications. It lets you hold the data centrally, available from any component, and makes debugging and testing easier. Its use is not always justified, however.\\n\\nAn analysis of the scenario\\nIn the scenario described — an SPA with authorization and one multi-step form — the application's state is relatively simple. Authorization normally holds a token and information about the user, while the form holds the data filled in over several steps. To pass the data between the form's steps you can use the local state of the parent component, or React Context. That requires no installation of additional libraries and does not complicate the architecture.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation without a state manager\\n\\nWhen a state manager is worth using\\n\\n- A great many components use one and the same data (a cart in an online shop, for example).\\n- Complex logic for synchronizing with a server (caching, optimistic updates).\\n- A need for debugging tools (the Redux DevTools).\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor simple SPAs with a limited amount of state, the use of a state manager is excessive. Local state and React context are a lighter, more comprehensible solution. A state manager is worth adopting only when the application grows and a real need for centralized state management appears.","// A form component with multiple steps\\nfunction MultiStepForm() {\\n  const [step, setStep] = useState(1);\\n  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({});\\n\\n  const handleNext = (data) => {\\n    setFormData(prev => ({ ...prev, ...data }));\\n    setStep(step + 1);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ token }}>\\n      {step === 1 && <Step1 onNext={handleNext} />}\\n      {step === 2 && <Step2 onNext={handleNext} />}\\n      {step === 3 && <Step3 data={formData} />}\\n    </AuthContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}"],[2505,"To hold a multi-step form's intermediate state, you can use the component's local state, useReducer or the Context API. Local state suits simple forms, useReducer complex logic, and the Context API access to the data from different components. You can also use Redux or libraries such as Formik and React Hook Form.","The main approaches to holding a multi-step form's state\\nWhen developing multi-step forms on the frontend, it matters to preserve the data between the steps, so that the user can go back or continue later. There are several ways of organizing such storage, each of which suits different scenarios.\\n\\nThe component's local state\\nThe simplest way is to hold all of the form's data in a single object inside the component that manages the steps. That is convenient for small forms with 2–3 steps.\\n\\nuseReducer for complex logic\\nIf the form has many fields and complex validation rules, it is better to use useReducer. That lets you handle the changes centrally and makes testing easier.\\n\\nThe Context API for global access\\nWhen the form is broken into separate components for each step, it is convenient to use the Context API, in order to avoid prop drilling. A context is created, which provides the form's data and the functions for updating it to all of the child components.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of method depends on the form's complexity and on the application's architecture. For simple forms local state is enough, for medium ones useReducer, and for large projects with a great many steps and components the Context API or Redux. That ensures convenience in the development and the maintenance of the code.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MultiStepForm() {\\n  const [step, setStep] = useState(1);\\n  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({\\n    name: '',\\n    email: '',\\n    address: ''\\n  });\\n\\n  const updateField = (field, value) => {\\n    setFormData(prev => ({ ...prev, [field]: value }));\\n  };\\n\\n  // The rendering of the steps depending on step\\n}\\n\\nconst initialState = { name: '', email: '', address: '' };\\n\\nfunction formReducer(state, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'UPDATE_FIELD':\\n      return { ...state, [action.field]: action.value };\\n    case 'RESET':\\n      return initialState;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction MultiStepForm() {\\n  const [formData, dispatch] = useReducer(formReducer, initialState);\\n}\\n\\nconst FormContext = React.createContext();\\n\\nfunction FormProvider({ children }) {\\n  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({});\\n  return (\\n    <FormContext.Provider value={{ formData, setFormData }}>\\n      {children}\\n    </FormContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}"],[2506,"For a small React project with corporate standards, it is best to use Webpack, since it is the most flexible and configurable bundler. It supports React out of the box through Babel and lets you integrate any plugins and loaders easily. If the company already has ready-made configurations or templates, Webpack will ensure they are compatible. Alternatives such as Vite or Parcel can be simpler, but they may not accord with the internal standards.","The choice of a bundler for a React project\\nWhen choosing a bundler for a small React project in conditions of corporate standards, the key factor becomes the compatibility with the existing infrastructure. Webpack is the de facto standard in large companies, thanks to its flexibility and its broad ecosystem of plugins. It lets you configure the build process precisely for any requirements, including optimization, code splitting and support for older browsers.\\n\\nA practical example of a Webpack configuration for React\\n\\nWhy Webpack rather than the alternatives\\n\\n- Compatibility with the standards: Webpack adapts easily to existing corporate configurations, to ESLint, Prettier and other tools.\\n- Flexibility: the ability to configure things finely through loaders and plugins, for working with TypeScript, CSS Modules, images and fonts.\\n- Performance: the built-in optimizations such as tree shaking and code splitting are critical for production builds.\\n\\nAlthough Vite offers faster development thanks to ESM, it may not support specific corporate plugins or may require additional configuration for compatibility. Parcel, for its part, is too automated and does not give enough control.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor a small React project in a company with established standards, Webpack remains the optimal choice, thanks to its flexibility, its maturity and its compatibility with the existing tools. It provides complete control over the build and integrates into any corporate environment easily.","// webpack.config.js\\nconst path = require('path');\\nconst HtmlWebpackPlugin = require('html-webpack-plugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  entry: './src/index.js',\\n  output: {\\n    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),\\n    filename: 'bundle.js',\\n  },\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.jsx?$/,\\n        exclude: /node_modules/,\\n        use: {\\n          loader: 'babel-loader',\\n          options: {\\n            presets: ['@babel/preset-env', '@babel/preset-react'],\\n          },\\n        },\\n      },\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.css$/,\\n        use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'],\\n      },\\n    ],\\n  },\\n  plugins: [\\n    new HtmlWebpackPlugin({\\n      template: './public/index.html',\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  resolve: {\\n    extensions: ['.js', '.jsx'],\\n  },\\n};"],[2507,"React uses a comparison (diffing) algorithm in order to update the DOM. When a list changes, React compares the old and the new virtual DOM trees. The keys help React identify the elements and minimize the repainting. Without keys React may recreate every element of the list.","How the reconciliation of lists works in React\\nWhen React updates a list of elements, it uses the reconciliation algorithm, which compares the virtual DOM trees before and after the change. That process is based on two assumptions: elements of different types create different trees, and keys let React identify the elements between renders.\\n\\nThe role of the keys\\nKeys are unique identifiers, which help React understand which element has been added, deleted or moved. Without keys React will compare the elements by index, which can lead to the DOM being updated wrongly and to the loss of the components' state.\\n\\nThe comparison algorithm\\nReact uses an O(n) algorithm, which goes through the list and compares the elements by key. If the keys match, React updates only the attributes that have changed. If a key has changed, React deletes the old element and creates a new one. That lets it update the DOM efficiently without a complete repaint.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing stable, unique keys in React lists is critically important for the performance and for the DOM being updated properly. That is especially useful when working with dynamic lists, where elements can be added, deleted or rearranged.","// An example with keys\\nconst items = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\\nreturn (\\n  <ul>\\n    {items.map((item, index) => (\\n      <li key={item}>{item}</li>\\n    ))}\\n  </ul>\\n);\\n\\n// Without keys React may recreate every element\\n{items.map((item, index) => (\\n  <li>{item}</li>\\n))}"],[2508,"React uses keys in order to identify the elements in an array. If the keys are not stated, React uses the element's index. When switching between different arrays of data, React can reuse the existing DOM nodes if their indexes match, which leads to the components' state being preserved. That happens because React does not recreate the component but updates its props.","Why do components preserve their state when there are no explicit keys?\\nWhen you render a list of components without stating explicit keys, React uses the element's index in the array as the key by default. That can lead to unexpected behaviour when switching between different arrays of data.\\n\\nThe reconciliation mechanism\\nReact compares the virtual DOM trees by means of a comparison algorithm. If the elements' keys match, React considers that it is one and the same element and updates only its props, preserving the component's state.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nIn this example, on a press of the button React reuses the existing InputComponent components, because their indexes (0 and 1) match. The state inside the components (the text typed, for example) will be preserved, even though the data has changed completely.\\n\\nThe right solution\\nAlways use unique, stable keys — identifiers from a database, for example:\\n\\nConclusion\\nExplicit keys are necessary for React to work properly when rendering lists. They help you avoid errors with the preservation of state and ensure the components are updated properly when the data changes.","function App() {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([{id: 1, text: 'A'}, {id: 2, text: 'B'}]);\\n  \\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {items.map((item, index) => (\\n        <InputComponent key={index} value={item.text} />\\n      ))}\\n      <button onClick={() => setItems([{id: 3, text: 'C'}, {id: 4, text: 'D'}])}>\\n        Change the data\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n{items.map(item => (\\n  <InputComponent key={item.id} value={item.text} />\\n))}"],[2509,"React Testing Library concentrates on testing components' behaviour from the user's point of view, working with the real DOM. Enzyme provides access to components' internal state and methods, which leads to the testing of implementation details. RTL encourages tests that are resistant to refactoring, whereas Enzyme makes them fragile.","The philosophy of testing\\nReact Testing Library (RTL) and Enzyme represent two different approaches to testing React components. RTL follows the principle: \\"The more your tests resemble the way your application is used, the more confidence they can give you\\". Enzyme, by contrast, provides lower-level access to a component's internals.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- The approach to finding elements: RTL uses queries based on accessibility (getByRole, getByText, getByLabelText), imitating the user's actions. Enzyme uses selectors (find('.class'), find(Component)).\\n- Access to the state: RTL provides no direct access to a component's state or props. Enzyme lets you call .state(), .props(), .instance().\\n- Testing implementation details: RTL actively discourages the testing of internal methods and state. Enzyme encourages it.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn the example RTL checks that the user sees '1' after a click. Enzyme checks the internal count state.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact Testing Library is preferable for creating reliable tests that do not break during a refactor. Enzyme can be useful for prototyping tests quickly, but its use often leads to fragile tests tied to the implementation.","// React Testing Library\\nimport { render, screen, fireEvent } from '@testing-library/react';\\nimport Counter from './Counter';\\n\\ntest('increments counter', () => {\\n  render(<Counter />);\\n  const button = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /increment/i });\\n  fireEvent.click(button);\\n  expect(screen.getByText('1')).toBeInTheDocument();\\n});\\n\\n// Enzyme\\nimport { shallow } from 'enzyme';\\nimport Counter from './Counter';\\n\\ntest('increments counter', () => {\\n  const wrapper = shallow(<Counter />);\\n  wrapper.find('button').simulate('click');\\n  expect(wrapper.state('count')).toBe(1);\\n});"],[2510,"To optimize a component that makes the page hang on large data, use the virtualization of the list (react-window, for example), lazy loading (React.lazy), memoization (React.memo, useMemo, useCallback) and pagination. You can also break the rendering into parts by means of requestAnimationFrame or Web Workers.","The problem and its causes\\nWhen a React component processes and renders a large volume of data (a list of thousands of items, for example), the browser can hang because the main thread is blocked. That happens because React performs the rendering synchronously, and the browser cannot update the interface until the JavaScript has finished its work.\\n\\nThe main approaches to optimization\\n\\n- The virtualization of the list — it renders only the visible elements, using libraries such as react-window or react-virtualized.\\n- Lazy loading — it breaks the code into chunks by means of React.lazy and Suspense.\\n- Memoization — it prevents repeat computations by means of React.memo, useMemo and useCallback.\\n- Pagination — it loads the data in portions, at the user's request.\\n- Breaking the rendering up — it uses requestAnimationFrame or setTimeout in order to distribute the work across the frames.\\n\\nAn example with virtualization\\n\\nAn example with memoization\\n\\nConclusion\\nApply virtualization for lists with thousands of elements, memoization for preventing redundant repaints, and lazy loading for reducing the initial bundle. These techniques are critical for creating responsive interfaces with large data.","import { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\\n\\nconst Row = ({ index, style }) => (\\n  <div style={style}>Item {index}</div>\\n);\\n\\nconst BigList = ({ items }) => (\\n  <List\\n    height={400}\\n    itemCount={items.length}\\n    itemSize={35}\\n    width={300}\\n  >\\n    {Row}\\n  </List>\\n);\\n\\nconst ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  // Heavy computations\\n  return <div>{data}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nconst Parent = ({ items }) => {\\n  const processed = useMemo(() => items.map(process), [items]);\\n  return processed.map(item => <ExpensiveComponent key={item.id} data={item} />);\\n};"],[2511,"The overflow problem arises when a dropdown list extends beyond the boundaries of its parent container. It can be solved by means of the CSS property overflow: visible on the parent, by using React portals (ReactDOM.createPortal) in order to render the list outside the container, or by applying libraries such as Popper.js for dynamic positioning. Setting a z-index and checking the screen's boundaries also help.","An understanding of the overflow problem\\nWhen a dropdown list is displayed inside a container with a limited height or with the overflow: hidden property, its content can be clipped. That is often met in modal windows, side panels or components with a fixed height. The problem is aggravated if the list has to appear beyond the bounds of the parent element.\\n\\nThe main approaches to solving it\\n\\n- The CSS solution: set overflow: visible on the parent container, if that does not break the design. It can break the scrolling or the layout, however.\\n- React Portals: use ReactDOM.createPortal in order to render the dropdown list in a separate DOM node (in the body, for example). That guarantees the list will not be limited by the parent's styles.\\n- Positioning libraries: Popper.js or Floating UI compute the list's optimal position automatically, avoiding overflow and the list extending beyond the screen's boundaries.\\n\\nAn example with a portal\\n\\nIn this example the list is rendered in the body, so the parent's overflow does not affect it. It also matters to manage the z-index and the closing on a click outside the list.\\n\\nAn example with Popper.js\\n\\nPopper.js adjusts the position automatically, so that the list does not extend beyond the visible area.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo solve overflow in dropdown lists, use portals if the parent's restrictions have to be removed entirely, or positioning libraries for dynamic adaptation. The CSS solutions suit only the simple cases, without deep nesting.","import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';\\n\\nfunction Dropdown({ isOpen, children }) {\\n  if (!isOpen) return null;\\n  return ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n    <div className=\\"dropdown-menu\\">{children}</div>,\\n    document.body\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport { usePopper } from 'react-popper';\\n\\nfunction Dropdown({ referenceElement }) {\\n  const [popperElement, setPopperElement] = useState(null);\\n  const { styles, attributes } = usePopper(referenceElement, popperElement);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div ref={setPopperElement} style={styles.popper} {...attributes.popper}>\\n      The list's content\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2512,"Drag-and-drop performance can degrade over time because of the accumulation of event handlers that have not been cancelled, especially during repeated drags. If the handlers are not removed once the drag has finished, they keep hanging in memory and being called, slowing things down. Memory leaks are also possible, because of new DOM elements being created without being cleaned up.","Why does drag-and-drop performance fall gradually?\\nThe problem is often related to how developers implement the drag mechanism. On every new drag-and-drop cycle (mousedown -> mousemove -> mouseup) new event handlers may be added without the old ones being removed. Over time the number of active listeners grows, and the browser spends more and more resources calling them, even when the drag is not active.\\n\\nThe main causes of the degradation\\n\\n- The accumulation of handlers: if mousemove and mouseup handlers are added on every mousedown but are not removed after the mouseup, they stay in memory.\\n- Memory leaks: the creation of temporary DOM elements (clones of the object being dragged, for example) without their subsequent removal leads to growing memory consumption.\\n- Unoptimized computations: the constant recomputation of the elements' positions, or the calling of expensive functions (getBoundingClientRect, for example) on every mousemove without caching.\\n\\nAn example of the problem and the solution\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo prevent a degradation of drag-and-drop performance, always remove the temporary event handlers once the drag has finished, use requestAnimationFrame in order to optimize the frequency of the updates, and avoid creating superfluous DOM elements without cleaning them up. That matters especially in applications with a long user session.","// The problematic code: the handlers are not removed\\nlet dragItem = document.getElementById('drag');\\ndragItem.addEventListener('mousedown', function(e) {\\n  function onMouseMove(e) {\\n    // expensive computations\\n    dragItem.style.left = e.clientX + 'px';\\n  }\\n  function onMouseUp() {\\n    document.removeEventListener('mousemove', onMouseMove);\\n    document.removeEventListener('mouseup', onMouseUp);\\n  }\\n  document.addEventListener('mousemove', onMouseMove);\\n  document.addEventListener('mouseup', onMouseUp);\\n});\\n\\n// The fixed code: the handlers are removed once it has finished\\ndragItem.addEventListener('mousedown', function(e) {\\n  const onMouseMove = (e) => {\\n    requestAnimationFrame(() => {\\n      dragItem.style.left = e.clientX + 'px';\\n    });\\n  };\\n  const onMouseUp = () => {\\n    document.removeEventListener('mousemove', onMouseMove);\\n    document.removeEventListener('mouseup', onMouseUp);\\n  };\\n  document.addEventListener('mousemove', onMouseMove);\\n  document.addEventListener('mouseup', onMouseUp);\\n});"],[2513,"The use of top/left causes a recomputation of the layout (a reflow) on every change, which reduces the performance. styled-components generates new CSS classes on every update of the styles, which aggravates the problem. It is recommended that you use the CSS property transform: translate() for moving elements, since it works at the level of the compositing and causes no reflow.","The problem of using top/left with styled-components in drag-and-drop\\nWhen implementing drag-and-drop, there is often a temptation to change an element's position through the CSS properties \`top\` and \`left\`. Such an approach has serious drawbacks, however, especially in combination with the styled-components library.\\n\\nWhy top/left is a bad choice\\nThe \`top\` and \`left\` properties relate to an element's geometry and affect the page's layout. On every change of them the browser performs a recomputation of the positions of all of the elements (a reflow), which is an expensive operation. In the context of drag-and-drop, where the position changes dozens of times a second, that leads to noticeable lag in the interface.\\n\\nThe particulars of styled-components\\nstyled-components generates CSS classes dynamically. When you pass changing values into the styles, the library creates a new class on every render. That loads React and the browser additionally, since the styles have to be recreated and applied to the DOM.\\n\\nAn example of the problematic code\\n\\nEvery movement of the mouse causes a re-render and the creation of a new CSS class.\\n\\nThe right solution: transform\\nThe property \`transform: translate(x, y)\` works at the level of the compositing — the browser moves the element without a recomputation of the layout. That is considerably faster. Besides, transform requires no creation of new CSS classes, so styled-components will not recreate the styles.\\n\\nThe fixed example\\n\\nNow the changes of the position happen smoothly, without redundant recomputations.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`transform: translate()\` for animations and the moving of elements, especially in drag-and-drop. That ensures high performance and avoids the problems with dynamic styles in styled-components.","const Draggable = styled.div\`\\n  position: absolute;\\n  top: \${props => props.y}px;\\n  left: \${props => props.x}px;\\n\`;\\n\\nfunction Component() {\\n  const [pos, setPos] = useState({x: 0, y: 0});\\n  \\n  const handleMouseMove = (e) => {\\n    setPos({x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY});\\n  };\\n  \\n  return <Draggable x={pos.x} y={pos.y} />;\\n}\\n\\nconst Draggable = styled.div\`\\n  position: absolute;\\n  transform: translate(\${props => props.x}px, \${props => props.y}px);\\n\`;\\n\\nfunction Component() {\\n  const [pos, setPos] = useState({x: 0, y: 0});\\n  \\n  const handleMouseMove = (e) => {\\n    setPos({x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY});\\n  };\\n  \\n  return <Draggable x={pos.x} y={pos.y} />;\\n}"],[2514,"The \\"Factory\\" pattern is applied on the frontend when objects (components, classes) with a common structure but different details have to be created. For generating modal windows, buttons or form elements depending on the type, for example. That makes the code simpler and more flexible.","What is the \\"Factory\\" pattern?\\nThe Factory pattern is a creational design pattern, which provides an interface for creating objects in a superclass but lets the subclasses change the type of the objects created. In frontend development it is especially useful when a great many elements of the same kind with different configurations have to be created.\\n\\nWhere to apply it on the frontend?\\n\\n- Creating UI components (buttons, modal windows, cards) on the basis of the type passed in.\\n- Generating form elements (input, select, checkbox) from a configuration.\\n- Creating objects for working with an API (different adapters for different versions).\\n\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe \\"Factory\\" pattern is worth applying when the code often creates objects with similar logic but different parameters. That reduces the duplication and makes the addition of new types of objects easier.","class ButtonFactory {\\n  createButton(type) {\\n    switch (type) {\\n      case 'primary':\\n        return new PrimaryButton();\\n      case 'secondary':\\n        return new SecondaryButton();\\n      default:\\n        return new DefaultButton();\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass PrimaryButton {\\n  render() { return '<button class=\\"primary\\">OK</button>'; }\\n}\\n\\nconst factory = new ButtonFactory();\\nconst btn = factory.createButton('primary');\\nconsole.log(btn.render()); // <button class=\\"primary\\">OK</button>"],[2515,"A loading state during asynchronous validation is normally added at the level of the field or of the form. Use an isLoading flag, which is set to true before the request begins and to false once it has completed. The loader is displayed next to the input field, so that the user understands a check is under way. It matters to block the submission of the form until the validation has completed, and to handle the errors.","The concept of asynchronous validation with a loader\\nAsynchronous validation is performed when the data has to be checked on the server — the uniqueness of a username, for example. During that time the user should see a loading indicator, in order to understand that the system is working. The loader is normally placed next to the input field or inside the submit button.\\n\\nWhere to add the loading state\\n\\n- At the level of the field: an isLoading flag for every field that requires an asynchronous check.\\n- At the level of the form: a general isSubmitting flag for the whole form, when the submission of the data requires validation.\\n\\nHow to implement it properly\\nUse the component's state (useState in React, for example) for holding the loading flag. Set it to true before the asynchronous function is called and to false once the response has arrived. Display a spinner or the text \\"Checking...\\" next to the field. An example in React:\\n\\nConclusion\\nAdding a loader during asynchronous validation improves the user experience, prevents repeat submissions and informs the user about the process. Apply this approach in registration and login forms, or in any other form where a check on the server is required.","const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);\\nconst [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\nconst validateUsername = async (value) => {\\n  setIsLoading(true);\\n  setError(null);\\n  try {\\n    const response = await fetch('/api/check-username', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ username: value }) });\\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Username taken');\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    setError(err.message);\\n  } finally {\\n    setIsLoading(false);\\n  }\\n};"],[2516,"The Observer pattern is used when one object (the subject) notifies a great many dependent objects (the observers) about changes of its state. In a user interface, for example, when the model's data changes, all of the related views are updated automatically. That implements loose coupling and makes maintaining the code easier.","What is the Observer pattern?\\nThe Observer pattern is a behavioural design pattern, which defines a one-to-many dependency between objects. When one object (the subject) changes its state, all of the dependent objects (the observers) are notified and updated automatically. That lets you avoid tight coupling and makes extending the system easier.\\n\\nWhere is it applied in real applications?\\nOne of the most widespread examples is event systems in user interfaces. In a web application in JavaScript, for example, when the data in the model changes (a list of tasks, for example), all of the components that display that data (a table and a counter, for example) have to update. The Observer pattern lets you subscribe those components to the model's changes and respond automatically.\\n\\nAn example of the code in JavaScript\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Observer pattern is useful in systems where a great many components have to be notified of changes automatically — in UI frameworks (React, Vue), in event systems, or when implementing pub/sub models. It improves the modularity and makes maintaining the code easier.","class Subject {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.observers = [];\\n  }\\n  subscribe(observer) {\\n    this.observers.push(observer);\\n  }\\n  unsubscribe(observer) {\\n    this.observers = this.observers.filter(obs => obs !== observer);\\n  }\\n  notify(data) {\\n    this.observers.forEach(observer => observer.update(data));\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Observer {\\n  constructor(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n  update(data) {\\n    console.log(\`\${this.name} received the data: \${data}\`);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst subject = new Subject();\\nconst observer1 = new Observer('Observer 1');\\nconst observer2 = new Observer('Observer 2');\\n\\nsubject.subscribe(observer1);\\nsubject.subscribe(observer2);\\nsubject.notify('A new message');\\n// The output: Observer 1 received the data: A new message\\n//             Observer 2 received the data: A new message"],[2517,"It is better to show the page partially, using skeletons or loading indicators for the individual blocks. That improves the perception of the speed and lets the user interact with the parts that have already loaded. Waiting for all of the data makes the UX worse and can lead to the loss of users.","The problem\\nWhen part of the data on a page depends on a slow external service, a dilemma arises: wait for the complete load or show partial content. Waiting for all of the data blocks the rendering and makes the user experience worse, especially on mobile devices or with an unstable connection.\\n\\nThe solution: partial loading\\nThe modern approach is to show the page at once with the data available, and to display skeletons (grey rectangles imitating the content's structure) or spinners for the slow blocks. That is called asynchronous rendering with progressive enhancement.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation in React\\n\\nWhen to apply it\\n\\n- If the pieces of data are independent — show them at once.\\n- If the data is critical to the navigation (a menu, for example) — you can show a skeleton, but do not block the whole page.\\n- For SEO and SSR, waiting may be required, but with the use of streaming.\\n\\nConclusion: partial loading with visual feedback (skeletons, spinners) is preferable to waiting completely, since it improves the perception of the performance and keeps the user on the page.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  const [orders, setOrders] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(setUser);\\n    fetch(\`/api/orders/\${userId}\`)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(setOrders);\\n  }, [userId]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {user ? <h1>{user.name}</h1> : <div className=\\"skeleton\\" />}\\n      {orders ? <OrderList items={orders} /> : <div className=\\"skeleton\\" />}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2518,"Yes, the previous validation result has to be reset on every new input of the user. That is necessary so that the user sees the current state of the errors rather than stale messages. If a field was marked as invalid and the user then corrected it, for example, the error message should disappear. Otherwise the user will be disoriented and will not understand that the error has already been corrected.","When developing forms with validation, it matters to update the state of the errors in good time. If the previous validation result is not reset on a new input, the user will see stale messages, which leads to confusion and a bad user experience.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- Real-time feedback: the user should see at once that their correction has been accepted by the system.\\n- The prevention of false errors: if a field was invalid and then became valid, the error message should disappear.\\n- An improved UX: the user will not spend time correcting problems that have already been solved.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\nLet us look at a simple form with email validation in React. On every change of the field we reset the previous error and start a new validation.\\n\\nIn this example, on every call of \`handleChange\` we first reset the error and then check the new value. If the user has typed a correct email, the error will not appear.\\n\\nConclusion\\nResetting the previous validation result on every new input is an obligatory practice for creating intuitive forms. It provides current feedback and prevents the user being misinformed.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction EmailForm() {\\n  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');\\n  const [error, setError] = useState('');\\n\\n  const validateEmail = (value) => {\\n    // We reset the previous error\\n    setError('');\\n    \\n    if (!value.includes('@')) {\\n      setError('The email must contain @');\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  const handleChange = (e) => {\\n    const value = e.target.value;\\n    setEmail(value);\\n    validateEmail(value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input\\n        type=\\"email\\"\\n        value={email}\\n        onChange={handleChange}\\n      />\\n      {error && <p style={{color: 'red'}}>{error}</p>}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2519,"It is better to show the items at once, but with an indication of the validation process. That improves the user experience, since the user sees instant feedback. If you wait for the complete validation, the interface will seem slow. The exception is critical data, where an error is impermissible.","Introduction\\nThe question concerns the balance between performance and the user experience when working with forms or lists of data. The main dilemma: show the items at once (an optimistic UI) or after complete validation (the conservative approach).\\n\\nThe optimistic UI\\nThis approach involves displaying the data immediately, even if it has not yet passed the check. When adding an item to a list, for example:\\n\\nThe advantages: the interface stays responsive and the user sees the result instantly. The disadvantages: temporarily incorrect data is possible.\\n\\nThe conservative approach\\nHere the data is displayed only after a successful validation. That guarantees the user will never see erroneous data, but it increases the waiting time.\\n\\nWhen to choose which\\n\\n- The optimistic UI — for data that is not critical (comments, likes, for example), where a temporary error is not frightening.\\n- The conservative one — for financial operations, medical data or any case where an error is impermissible.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe optimal strategy is a hybrid: show the items at once with an indication of the validation status, but for critical data use a blocking of the interface until the confirmation arrives.","function addItem(item) {\\n  // We add it to the state at once\\n  setItems(prev => [...prev, { ...item, status: 'pending' }]);\\n  \\n  // The asynchronous validation\\n  validateItem(item)\\n    .then(validated => {\\n      setItems(prev => prev.map(i => \\n        i.id === item.id ? { ...validated, status: 'valid' } : i\\n      ));\\n    })\\n    .catch(error => {\\n      setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== item.id));\\n      showError(error);\\n    });\\n}\\n\\nasync function addItem(item) {\\n  try {\\n    const validated = await validateItem(item);\\n    setItems(prev => [...prev, validated]);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    showError(error);\\n  }\\n}"],[2520,"The \\"Command\\" pattern turns a request or an operation into a separate object, containing all of the information needed to perform it. That lets you parameterize objects with operations, put requests in a queue, log them and support cancellation. On the frontend it is often used for implementing a history of actions (undo/redo), macros and asynchronous queues.","What is the \\"Command\\" pattern?\\nThe Command pattern is a behavioural design pattern, which turns a request into a self-contained object. That object contains all of the data needed for performing the action: a reference to the receiver, the parameters and the method for performing it. Such an approach separates the sender of the request from its executor, which gives flexibility in managing the operations.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nThe pattern's main participants:\\n- Command — the interface with an execute() method and, optionally, an undo().\\n- ConcreteCommand — the particular implementation, which binds the action to the receiver.\\n- Invoker — the initiator, which holds the command and calls for it to be performed.\\n- Receiver — the object that performs the business logic directly.\\n\\nAn example in JavaScript for the frontend\\nLet us look at a simple text editor with the ability to undo actions:\\n\\nWhere is it applied on the frontend?\\n\\n- Undo/Redo — in text editors, graphics editors, forms.\\n- Macros and scripts — recording a sequence of actions for a repeat performance.\\n- Queues of asynchronous operations — sending several requests with the ability to roll back, for example.\\n- Components with deferred execution — buttons that put a command in a queue instead of acting immediately.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe \\"Command\\" pattern is useful when the initiation of an operation has to be separated from its performance, especially if support for cancellation, logging or queueing is required. On the frontend it is indispensable for creating interactive interfaces with a history of actions and flexible management of the user's operations.","class Command {\\n  execute() {}\\n  undo() {}\\n}\\n\\nclass InsertTextCommand extends Command {\\n  constructor(receiver, text) {\\n    super();\\n    this.receiver = receiver;\\n    this.text = text;\\n  }\\n  execute() {\\n    this.receiver.insert(this.text);\\n  }\\n  undo() {\\n    this.receiver.deleteLast(this.text.length);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass TextEditor {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.content = '';\\n    this.history = [];\\n  }\\n  insert(text) {\\n    this.content += text;\\n  }\\n  deleteLast(n) {\\n    this.content = this.content.slice(0, -n);\\n  }\\n  executeCommand(command) {\\n    command.execute();\\n    this.history.push(command);\\n  }\\n  undo() {\\n    const command = this.history.pop();\\n    if (command) command.undo();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst editor = new TextEditor();\\neditor.executeCommand(new InsertTextCommand(editor, 'Hello'));\\neditor.executeCommand(new InsertTextCommand(editor, ' World'));\\nconsole.log(editor.content); // 'Hello World'\\neditor.undo();\\nconsole.log(editor.content); // 'Hello'"],[2521,"On a click on a button in React the setState function is called, which updates the component's state. That leads to a re-render of the component itself and of all of its child components, if they are not optimized by means of React.memo or PureComponent. The re-render happens because React compares the virtual DOM and updates the real DOM only where there are changes.","How a re-render works in React\\nWhen the user clicks on a button, the event handler is called, which updates the state through setState. React marks the component as 'dirty' and schedules a re-render. During the re-render React calls the component's function (or the render method for class components) and compares the virtual DOM obtained with the previous one.\\n\\nWhich components will re-render\\n\\n- The component in which the change of state happened always re-renders.\\n- All of its child components also re-render by default, even if their props have not changed.\\n- If a child component is wrapped in React.memo or is a PureComponent, it will re-render only when its props change.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example, on a click on the button Parent will re-render, but Child will not re-render, thanks to React.memo, since its props have not changed.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of the re-render mechanism helps you optimize the performance of React applications, especially when working with large trees of components. Use React.memo, useMemo and useCallback in order to prevent redundant re-renders.","function Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click</button>\\n      <Child />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(() => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <div>Child</div>\\n});"],[2522,"Yes, the child component will render afresh on every typing of text in the input. That happens because a change of state (through useState) in the parent component causes a repeat render of it, which by default leads to a repeat render of all of the child components. React does not optimize that automatically unless React.memo or other methods are used.","Why does a repeat render of the child component happen?\\nWhen the user types text in the input, the onChange event handler updates the parent component's state by means of setState (through useState, for example). The change of state starts a repeat render of the parent, and React by default repaints all of its child components recursively, even if their props have not changed. That is React's standard behaviour for ensuring the UI stays current.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example, on every typing of text the message 'Child re-rendered' will appear in the console, which confirms the repeat render of the child component.\\n\\nHow is this applied in practice?\\nSuch behaviour can be undesirable for heavy components. For optimization React.memo is used, which prevents a repeat render if the props have not changed. You can also move the state into a separate component or use useMemo/useCallback for stable references.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of this mechanism matters for optimizing the performance of React applications. Use React.memo for child components that do not depend on the state that is changing, in order to avoid redundant renders.","function Parent() {\\n  const [text, setText] = useState('');\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={text} onChange={e => setText(e.target.value)} />\\n      <Child />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Child() {\\n  console.log('Child re-rendered');\\n  return <div>Static content</div>;\\n}"],[2523,"So that a child component does not render when text is typed in an input, wrap it in React.memo. That will prevent the re-render if the props have not changed. Also make sure the callback functions passed into the child component are wrapped in useCallback, so that their references stay stable.","The problem of redundant re-renders\\nIn React, when a parent component's state changes (when text is typed in an input, for example), all of its child components re-render by default. That can lead to performance problems, especially if the child component performs heavy computations or contains complex nesting.\\n\\nThe solution with React.memo\\nReact.memo is a higher-order component (HOC), which remembers the result of the rendering. It compares the new props with the previous ones by means of a shallow comparison. If the props have not changed, React.memo skips the re-render and uses the cached result.\\n\\nThe importance of useCallback\\nIf you pass a function in directly (without useCallback), a new reference to the function will be created on every render of the parent. React.memo will see the change of the onClick prop and will perform a re-render, despite the wrapper. useCallback guarantees the reference to the function will stay the same if the dependencies have not changed.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse React.memo for child components that should not respond to changes of the parent's state, and combine it with useCallback for function props. That is the standard approach to optimizing the performance of React applications, especially when working with forms, lists or animations.","import React, { useState, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ExpensiveChild = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click me</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [text, setText] = useState('');\\n  \\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    console.log('Button clicked');\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={text} onChange={e => setText(e.target.value)} />\\n      <ExpensiveChild onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2524,"A useEffect with a dependency runs after the render, when the value stated has changed, and is intended for synchronization with the outside world. An event handler is called immediately on the user's action. A useEffect suits requests and logging, while a handler suits validation, the submission of a form and animations.","The difference between a useEffect and an event handler\\nIn React side effects can be performed in two main ways: through a \`useEffect\` (declaratively) and through event handlers (imperatively). An understanding of the difference is critical for the proper management of state and of the performance.\\n\\nWhen the code runs\\n\\n- A useEffect is started after React has committed the changes to the DOM. If an array of dependencies is stated, the effect runs only when those values change. That is asynchronous relative to the render.\\n- An event handler (\`onClick\`, for example) is called synchronously at the moment of the user's action, before the next render.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- A useEffect — for synchronization with external systems (requests, subscriptions, timers), when you have to respond to changes of state or props.\\n- An event handler — for an immediate reaction to the user's actions (validation, the submission of a form, animations, the updating of local state).\\n\\nConclusion: use a useEffect for declarative side effects that have to happen after the render, and event handlers for imperative actions that require an instant response.","import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Example() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // useEffect: it runs after the render, when count has changed\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Count changed to', count);\\n    // A request to an API can be made here\\n  }, [count]);\\n\\n  // The handler: it is called at once on a click\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    console.log('Button clicked, current count:', count);\\n    setCount(count + 1);\\n  };\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>;\\n}"],[2525,"Apart from useEffect, you can use useLayoutEffect for a synchronous run after the DOM mutations, a callback ref for direct access to the DOM node on mounting, a MutationObserver for tracking the appearance of an element, and the autofocus attribute in HTML. These approaches let you avoid redundant renders and improve the performance.","The alternative ways of autofocus\\nAutofocus on an element when it appears in the DOM can be implemented in several ways, apart from the standard useEffect. Each of them has its own particulars and areas of application.\\n\\n1. useLayoutEffect\\nThis hook runs synchronously after all of the DOM mutations but before the browser paints the changes. That guarantees the focus will be set before the user sees the element.\\n\\n2. A callback ref\\nA callback ref lets you perform an action immediately after the element mounts. That is a more flexible approach, which requires no additional effects.\\n\\n3. A MutationObserver\\nFor elements that are added dynamically you can use a MutationObserver, which tracks the changes in the DOM and sets the focus when the element you need appears.\\n\\n4. The HTML autofocus attribute\\nThe simplest way is to use the built-in autofocus attribute, which works without JavaScript but is not supported by every browser and can conflict with React.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of method depends on the context: useLayoutEffect suits a synchronous focus, a callback ref the simple cases, a MutationObserver dynamic elements, and autofocus static HTML. Use these approaches in order to optimize the performance and to avoid redundant renders.","import { useLayoutEffect, useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction AutoFocusInput() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useLayoutEffect(() => {\\n    inputRef.current?.focus();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <input ref={inputRef} />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction AutoFocusInput() {\\n  const inputRef = useCallback((node) => {\\n    node?.focus();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <input ref={inputRef} />;\\n}\\n\\nconst observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {\\n  mutations.forEach((mutation) => {\\n    mutation.addedNodes.forEach((node) => {\\n      if (node.matches?.('input')) {\\n        node.focus();\\n        observer.disconnect();\\n      }\\n    });\\n  });\\n});\\n\\nobserver.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });\\n\\n<input autofocus />"],[2526,"On frontend projects I prefer a modular folder architecture based on features (feature-based). In it every module contains all of the components, styles, tests and logic related to it. That makes navigating, scaling and maintaining the code easier. The 'auth' folder, for example, includes the components, hooks, API requests and utilities for the authorization.","The choice of a folder architecture on frontend projects\\nThe folder architecture directly affects the maintainability, the scalability and the speed of development. The most effective approach for medium and large projects is a feature-based (modular) architecture. It groups the code by functional capability rather than by technical role.\\n\\nWhy feature-based?\\n\\n- Isolation: every module is self-sufficient, which makes refactoring and the removal of functionality easier.\\n- Scaling: new features are added without changing the existing structure.\\n- Navigation: a developer finds the code they need quickly, since all of the related files are next to one another.\\n\\nAn example of the structure\\n\\nConclusion\\nA feature-based architecture is recommended for projects where long-term maintenance and teamwork matter. It reduces the coupling of the code and speeds the onboarding of new developers up.","src/\\n  features/\\n    auth/\\n      components/\\n        LoginForm.tsx\\n        RegisterForm.tsx\\n      hooks/\\n        useAuth.ts\\n      api/\\n        authApi.ts\\n      utils/\\n        validateEmail.ts\\n      tests/\\n        LoginForm.test.tsx\\n    dashboard/\\n      components/\\n        Chart.tsx\\n        StatsCard.tsx\\n      hooks/\\n        useDashboardData.ts\\n  shared/\\n    ui/\\n      Button.tsx\\n      Modal.tsx\\n    utils/\\n      formatDate.ts\\n  app/\\n    App.tsx\\n    routes.tsx"],[2527,"To find excessive re-renders, people use the React DevTools Profiler, which shows which components repaint and why. You can also use console.log or flags in the components. Tools such as Why Did You Render help you reveal unnecessary re-renders automatically. The optimization includes React.memo, useMemo and useCallback.","The tools for revealing excessive re-renders\\nThe main tool is the React DevTools Profiler. It records a session of interaction with the application and shows which components repainted, how long that took, and which props or state changed. That lets you find the problem areas quickly.\\n\\nThe practical approaches\\n\\n- React.memo — it wraps a component, preventing a re-render if the props have not changed (a shallow comparison).\\n- useMemo and useCallback — they memoize values and functions, so that the references to them stay stable between renders.\\n- A proper structure of the state — lift the state only to the level needed, and avoid holding computed data in the state.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example \`ExpensiveComponent\` repaints only when \`text\` changes rather than on every click on the button, thanks to the \`React.memo\` and the \`useMemo\`.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the React DevTools Profiler for the initial analysis, and then apply memoization and the optimization of the components' structure. That is especially useful in large applications with frequent updates of the data, in order to avoid a collapse in the performance.","import React, { useState, useMemo } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  console.log('Render ExpensiveComponent');\\n  return <div>{data}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const [text, setText] = useState('');\\n\\n  // The memoization, so that the reference does not change when count changes\\n  const memoizedData = useMemo(() => text.toUpperCase(), [text]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>\\n      <input value={text} onChange={e => setText(e.target.value)} />\\n      <ExpensiveComponent data={memoizedData} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2528,"Caching in a frontend application can be implemented at several levels: the HTTP cache (through the Cache-Control headers), the browser's cache (localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB), the Service Worker's cache (the Cache API) and the CDN's cache. Each level solves its own task: the HTTP cache reduces the number of requests to the server, a Service Worker lets you cache resources for offline mode, and localStorage/IndexedDB hold the application's data. The choice of level depends on the type of data and on the requirements on its currency.","The levels of caching in a frontend application\\nCaching on the client side is a key technique for speeding the loading of pages up and reducing the load on the server. It lets you hold copies of resources (HTML, CSS, JS, images, API data) locally, so that on repeat requests they are not downloaded again. Let us look at the main levels of caching.\\n\\nThe HTTP cache (Cache-Control, ETag)\\nThis level is managed by the headers of the server's response. The browser saves the responses in its internal cache automatically and uses them until their lifetime expires. An example of the configuration on the server (Node.js/Express):\\n\\nHere the resource will be cached for 1 hour. To check its currency, an ETag or Last-Modified is used.\\n\\nThe Service Worker's cache (the Cache API)\\nA Service Worker is a proxy layer between the browser and the network. It can intercept requests and return cached responses, even when there is no internet. An example of caching the static files:\\n\\nThat lets the application work offline and speeds the loading up on a weak connection.\\n\\nThe cache at the level of the application (localStorage, IndexedDB)\\nFor holding the application's data (the user's settings, the results of an API, for example) the Web Storage API or IndexedDB is used. An example with localStorage:\\n\\nIndexedDB suits large volumes of structured data.\\n\\nThe CDN's cache\\nA Content Delivery Network (CDN) caches static resources on geographically distributed servers. That is not quite a client-side cache, but it affects the loading time. The configuration normally happens on the CDN provider's side.\\n\\nConclusion: caching in a frontend application should be applied in order to improve the user experience and reduce the load on the server. The HTTP cache suits static files, a Service Worker offline mode and dynamic data, and localStorage/IndexedDB the application's data. The choice of level depends on the particular requirements on the currency and the volume of the data.","app.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {\\n  res.set('Cache-Control', 'public, max-age=3600');\\n  res.json({ data: 'some data' });\\n});\\n\\nself.addEventListener('install', event => {\\n  event.waitUntil(\\n    caches.open('v1').then(cache => {\\n      return cache.addAll(['/index.html', '/style.css', '/app.js']);\\n    })\\n  );\\n});\\n\\nself.addEventListener('fetch', event => {\\n  event.respondWith(\\n    caches.match(event.request).then(response => {\\n      return response || fetch(event.request);\\n    })\\n  );\\n});\\n\\n// Saving\\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify({ name: 'John' }));\\n\\n// Reading\\nconst user = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));"],[2529,"Yes, the effect will be triggered on every render if a spread object is passed into the dependencies. The reason is that the spread operator creates a new object every time it renders, and React compares the dependencies by reference. A new object always has a new reference, so the useEffect will run on every render even if the object's fields have not changed.","The problem with a spread object in useEffect's dependencies\\nWhen you pass a spread object into useEffect's array of dependencies, you create a new object on every render. React uses a strict comparison (===) in order to check the dependencies. Since every new object has a unique reference in memory, React considers that the dependency has changed and starts the effect afresh.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the effect will run on every render, even if data has not changed. That happens because { ...data } creates a new object with a new reference.\\n\\nThe right solution\\nTo avoid this problem, pass the object's particular fields into the dependencies:\\n\\nOr use a stable reference to the object, if it does not change:\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the spread operator in useEffect's dependencies only if you really do have to respond to every change of the object. In most cases it is better to pass the particular fields or to use a useMemo in order to stabilize the reference.","const MyComponent = ({ data }) => {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Effect runs');\\n  }, [{ ...data }]); // A new object every time\\n\\n  return <div>{data.name}</div>;\\n};\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  console.log('Effect runs only when name changes');\\n}, [data.name]); // Only the particular field\\n\\nconst stableData = useMemo(() => data, [data]);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  console.log('Effect runs');\\n}, [stableData]);"],[2530,"Use useRef for holding the prop's previous value. Inside a useEffect, update ref.current after you have used it for the comparison. That lets you obtain the previous value on the next render.","How to obtain a prop's previous value by means of useRef\\nIn React there is no built-in way of obtaining a prop's previous value, but it can be done by means of the useRef hook together with useEffect. useRef creates an object that preserves its value between renders without causing a repeat render when it changes.\\n\\nThe implementation\\nCreate a ref, which will hold the previous value. Inside a useEffect, update ref.current after you have used it. Thus on the next render ref.current will contain the previous value.\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\n- useRef creates a ref with the initial value undefined.\\n- The useEffect is started after every render, when value changes.\\n- Inside the useEffect we update ref.current, but that does not cause a repeat render.\\n- On the next render usePrevious returns the previous value, since ref.current has not yet been updated.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThis pattern is useful for comparing the current and the previous values of props — for animations, logging or conditional effects, for example. It is simple to implement and requires no additional libraries.","import { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction usePrevious(value) {\\n  const ref = useRef();\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    ref.current = value;\\n  }, [value]);\\n  return ref.current;\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent({ count }) {\\n  const prevCount = usePrevious(count);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The current one: {count}</p>\\n      <p>The previous one: {prevCount}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2531,"Passing a function into useState lets you perform the initialization of the state only once, on the first render. That is useful when the initial value requires complex computation or a read from localStorage. Unlike the passing of a value, the function will not be called on every update of the component, which improves the performance.","What is lazy initialization in useState?\\nIn React the useState hook can take not only a value but also a function. When a function is passed in, React calls it only once — on the component's first render. That is called lazy initialization. The result of the call of the function becomes the initial state.\\n\\nWhen is that needed?\\nLazy initialization is useful when the initial state requires costly operations: a read from localStorage, complex mathematical computations, the parsing of large data. Without it those operations would be performed on every render, even if the state had already been initialized.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe difference from passing a value\\nIf you pass a value in, it will be computed on every render (if it is an expression). A function, on the other hand, is called only once. That matters especially for heavy operations, or when the initial value depends on props that can change.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse lazy initialization in useState when the initial state requires expensive computations or asynchronous operations. That improves the performance and makes the code more predictable.","// Bad: the computation on every render\\nconst [state, setState] = useState(expensiveComputation());\\n\\n// Good: the computation only once\\nconst [state, setState] = useState(() => expensiveComputation());\\n\\n// An example with localStorage\\nconst [theme, setTheme] = useState(() => {\\n  const saved = localStorage.getItem('theme');\\n  return saved ? saved : 'light';\\n});"],[2532,"Any value can be put in useState: a primitive (a number, a string, a boolean), an object, an array, null, undefined or a function. If a function is passed in, React will call it only on the first render — that is called lazy initialization. You cannot pass a Promise or other asynchronous values in directly.","What can be passed into useState's initialValue?\\nThe \`useState\` hook in React takes the state's initial value. That can be any JavaScript data type: primitives (numbers, strings, booleans), objects, arrays, \`null\`, \`undefined\` or even a function. The main rule is that the value has to be synchronous rather than asynchronous (a Promise will not do, for example).\\n\\nLazy initialization\\nIf the initial state requires complex computation, you can pass a function in. React will call it only once, on the component's first render, which improves the performance. For example:\\n\\nThat is especially useful when the initial value is obtained from localStorage or from an API (but synchronously).\\n\\nExamples of the usage\\n\\n- Primitives: \`useState(0)\`, \`useState('hello')\`, \`useState(true)\`\\n- Objects: \`useState({ name: 'John', age: 30 })\`\\n- Arrays: \`useState([1, 2, 3])\`\\n- null/undefined: \`useState(null)\`\\n- A function: \`useState(() => 42)\`\\n\\nWhat cannot be passed in?\\nYou cannot pass asynchronous values in, such as a Promise or the result of a call of an asynchronous function. If data has to be loaded from a server, use a \`useEffect\` in order to set the state after the mounting.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`useState\` with any synchronous value. For expensive computations, apply lazy initialization through a function. That is standard practice in React for managing components' state.","const [state, setState] = useState(() => {\\n  const initial = expensiveComputation();\\n  return initial;\\n});"],[2533,"Hooks in React run synchronously during the rendering of the component. That means a call of a hook such as useState or useEffect happens in the same thread as the rendering. The state update caused by a hook can be deferred and handled asynchronously in the next render, however. It matters to remember that the hooks themselves are not asynchronous, but their effects can be.","The synchronous nature of the hooks\\nHooks in React, such as useState, useEffect, useContext and the others, run synchronously during the rendering of the component. That means that when you call a hook, its logic is handled immediately, in the same thread of execution as the component's function. React guarantees that the order of the calls of the hooks is preserved between renders, which lets it bind the state and the effects properly.\\n\\nAn example of a synchronous run\\n\\nIn this example useState is called synchronously, and the count value is available immediately after the call. setCount, which updates the state, works asynchronously, however: React schedules a new render but does not perform it instantly.\\n\\nThe asynchronous aspects\\nAlthough the hooks themselves are synchronous, some of their effects can be asynchronous. A useEffect can contain asynchronous operations such as requests to an API, for example. But the useEffect hook itself is called synchronously after the rendering, while its callback runs later.\\n\\nHere the useEffect registers the effect synchronously, but the fetch runs asynchronously.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact's hooks are synchronous by nature, which ensures predictability and the order of execution. The asynchrony arises only in the effects' callbacks, or when the state updates, which is scheduled for the next render. That matters to take into account during development, in order to avoid errors related to expecting instant changes of the state.","function Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // synchronously\\n  console.log('Render:', count); // it runs at once\\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;\\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  fetch('/data').then(response => response.json()); // asynchronously\\n}, []);"],[2534,"A stepper component displays a sequence of steps. The length prop sets the overall number of steps, while activeStep points to the current active step. The component highlights the active step visually and shows the user's progress. That makes navigating multi-step forms or processes easier.","What is a stepper component?\\nA Stepper component is a UI element, which visualizes a sequence of steps in a process — in a multi-step form or a set-up wizard, for example. It helps the user understand which stage they are at and how many steps are left.\\n\\nThe length and activeStep props\\nThe \`length\` prop determines the overall number of steps in the process. The \`activeStep\` prop states the index of the current active step (starting from 0). The component uses those values in order to display the step indicators, highlight the active step and possibly mark the completed steps.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\n\\nThe application\\nSteppers are widely used in registration forms, checkout processes, the set-up of applications and any scenario in which actions have to be performed step by step. They improve the user experience by making the process transparent and manageable.\\n\\nConclusion: a stepper component with the length and activeStep props is a simple, effective way of visualizing the progress in multi-step interfaces. It is worth applying everywhere the user has to perform several steps in sequence, in order to reduce the cognitive load and improve the convenience.","function Stepper({ length, activeStep }) {\\n  const steps = Array.from({ length }, (_, i) => i);\\n  return (\\n    <div style={{ display: 'flex', gap: '8px' }}>\\n      {steps.map((step) => (\\n        <div\\n          key={step}\\n          style={{\\n            width: '30px',\\n            height: '30px',\\n            borderRadius: '50%',\\n            backgroundColor: step === activeStep ? '#007bff' : '#ccc',\\n            display: 'flex',\\n            alignItems: 'center',\\n            justifyContent: 'center',\\n            color: '#fff',\\n          }}\\n        >\\n          {step + 1}\\n        </div>\\n      ))}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2535,"The \`<dialog>\` element in HTML has an open attribute, which shows the dialog. In React, managing it through an open prop means the component controls the dialog's visibility by means of state. When open equals true, the dialog is displayed; otherwise it is hidden. To close it you can use the close() method or change the state.","Managing it through an open prop\\nThe \`<dialog>\` element is a native HTML element for creating modal windows. In React, managing its visibility through an open prop lets you make the component controlled: the parent's state determines whether the dialog is open or not.\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe open prop is a boolean value. When it is true the dialog is displayed; when it is false it is hidden. Simply setting the open attribute is not enough for a modal window to work properly, however: you also have to use the show() or showModal() methods for the opening and close() for the closing. In React that is done through refs and effects.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe application\\nSuch an approach is used for creating modal windows, popups or confirmation dialogs. The open prop gives the parent component complete control over the visibility, which makes managing the state easier.\\n\\nConclusion: use management through an open prop when the dialog's state has to be synchronized with external data — when opening it on a click or after a load, for example.","import React, { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Dialog({ open, onClose, children }) {\\n  const dialogRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const dialog = dialogRef.current;\\n    if (open) {\\n      dialog.showModal(); // or show() for a non-modal one\\n    } else {\\n      dialog.close();\\n    }\\n  }, [open]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <dialog ref={dialogRef} onClose={onClose}>\\n      {children}\\n      <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>\\n    </dialog>\\n  );\\n}"],[2536,"The \`<dialog>\` element provides the showModal() and close() methods for managing the display. In React you have to obtain access to the DOM element through useRef and then call those methods in the event handlers. That lets you create modal windows without third-party libraries.","What the <dialog> element is\\nThe \`<dialog>\` element is a native HTML element intended for creating modal windows and dialogs. It provides the built-in methods showModal() for opening the window in modal mode (blocking the background) and close() for closing it. That makes the development easier, since it requires no additional libraries.\\n\\nThe use in React through refs\\nIn React the useRef hook is used for access to a DOM element. A ref is created, which is bound to the \`<dialog>\` element. Then in the event handlers (onClick, for example) the showModal() and close() methods are called through the ref's current value.\\n\\nThe particulars and the application\\nThe showModal() method opens the dialog over the rest of the content and blocks the interaction with the background. To close it you can use close() or a press of the Escape key. The element also supports the 'close' event, which can be handled for additional logic. This approach is useful for simple modal windows, notifications or forms, when complex animation or customization is not required.\\n\\nConclusion: use \`<dialog>\` with refs in React for the fast creation of modal windows without external dependencies, if the project requires no complex state management or styling.","import { useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Modal() {\\n  const dialogRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const openModal = () => {\\n    dialogRef.current?.showModal();\\n  };\\n\\n  const closeModal = () => {\\n    dialogRef.current?.close();\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={openModal}>Open Modal</button>\\n      <dialog ref={dialogRef}>\\n        <p>Modal content</p>\\n        <button onClick={closeModal}>Close</button>\\n      </dialog>\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[2537,"React.memo takes a comparison function (areEqual) as its optional second argument. It receives the previous and the new props and has to return true if the props are equal (no re-render is needed) and false if they are different (a re-render is needed). By default a shallow comparison is used.","What React.memo is, and what the second argument is for\\nReact.memo is a higher-order component (HOC), which memoizes a functional component. It prevents a repeat rendering if the props have not changed. By default React.memo uses a shallow comparison of the props. In some cases, however, custom comparison logic is required — and that is what the second argument exists for.\\n\\nThe second argument: the areEqual function\\nThe second argument is a function, which takes two parameters: prevProps and nextProps. It has to return true if the props count as equal (no re-render is needed) and false if they are different (a re-render is needed). If no function is passed in, React.memo uses the standard shallow comparison.\\n\\nWhen is that useful?\\n\\n- When the props contain objects or arrays that are created afresh on every render of the parent, while their content does not change.\\n- When changes of particular props have to be ignored.\\n- For optimizing the performance in components with heavy computations.\\n\\nThe important notes\\n\\n- The comparison function is called only when the props change. If the component re-renders for another reason (because of a change of state, for example), the memoization is not applied.\\n- You should not abuse custom comparison — it can itself be expensive.\\n- If the comparison function returns false too often, the memoization loses its point.\\n\\nConclusion: React.memo's second argument is an areEqual function for precise control over when the component should re-render. Use it when a shallow comparison is not enough — when working with nested objects or arrays, for example — in order to avoid redundant re-renders and improve the performance.","const MyComponent = React.memo(\\n  (props) => <div>{props.value}</div>,\\n  (prevProps, nextProps) => {\\n    // The custom comparison logic\\n    return prevProps.value === nextProps.value;\\n  }\\n);"],[2538,"To initialize an array in useState, pass the array in as the initial value: const [items, setItems] = useState([]). If the array should be empty, use an empty array []. For a pre-filled array, pass it in directly: useState([1, 2, 3]). Avoid passing a function that returns an array unless lazy initialization is required.","The initialization of an array in useState\\nIn React the useState hook lets you hold a component's state. For an array the initial value is passed in as the argument. The simplest case is an empty array: \`useState([])\`. If the array has to contain data, pass it in directly: \`useState([1, 2, 3])\`.\\n\\nLazy initialization\\nIf the initial state requires complex computation, use an initializer function: \`useState(() => computeInitialArray())\`. That guarantees the computation will be performed only once, on the first render.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe important points\\n\\n- Do not change the array directly (push, splice) — use the methods that return a new array (spread, concat, filter, map).\\n- To update the state, always pass in a new value, or a function returning a new value.\\n- When working with objects inside the array, create copies of the objects when changing them.\\n\\nConclusion: the proper initialization of an array in useState is the passing of an initial value (empty or filled), or of a function for lazy initialization. That is the basis of working with collections of data in React.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TodoList() {\\n  const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]); // an empty array\\n  const [initialTodos] = useState(() => {\\n    // the lazy initialization\\n    return ['Learn React', 'Build project'];\\n  });\\n\\n  const addTodo = (text) => {\\n    setTodos([...todos, text]); // the addition of an element\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <ul>\\n        {todos.map((todo, index) => (\\n          <li key={index}>{todo}</li>\\n        ))}\\n      </ul>\\n      <button onClick={() => addTodo('New task')}>Add</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2539,"The problem is that setInterval creates a closure over the state's value as it was at the moment the interval was created. To use the current value, you can pass a function into setState (prev => prev + 1) or use useRef for holding the current value. You can also recreate the interval when a dependency changes, by means of useEffect.","The problem with closures in setInterval\\nWhen you use setInterval inside a useEffect, the callback function creates a closure over the state variable that was current at the moment the interval was created. That means that even if the state updates, the interval keeps using the old value.\\n\\nThe solution with a functional update\\nThe simplest way is to use the functional form of setState, which receives the previous state and returns the new one:\\n\\nThe solution with useRef\\nAnother approach is to hold the current value in a ref and read it inside the interval:\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse a functional update of the state for the simple cases, or useRef when the current value has to be read without recreating the interval. That guarantees the timers work properly and avoids bugs with stale data.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const id = setInterval(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + 1); // it always uses the current value\\n  }, 1000);\\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\\n}, []);\\n\\nconst [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\nconst countRef = useRef(count);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  countRef.current = count;\\n}, [count]);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const id = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log(countRef.current); // always current\\n  }, 1000);\\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\\n}, []);"],[2540,"TanStack Query (React Query) specializes in managing server state: the caching, the synchronization and the updating of data from an API. Redux is a universal state manager, which is often used for client state, but for server state it requires a great deal of boilerplate code to be written (reducers, actions, thunks). TanStack Query handles the cache, the background refetching and the invalidation automatically, while Redux leaves that to the developer.","The main difference\\nTanStack Query (formerly React Query) and Redux solve different tasks, although both manage state. TanStack Query was created specifically for working with server state — the data that arrives from an API and can go stale. It provides built-in mechanisms for caching, background refetching, pagination and invalidation. Redux is a universal container for state, which draws no distinction between server and client data. To work with an API in Redux you have to configure middleware by hand (Redux Thunk or Redux Saga, for example) and write reducers for every request.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\nTanStack Query:\\n\\nRedux with Thunk:\\n\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- TanStack Query — for any application that interacts with an API actively. It cuts the code down, updates the data automatically and manages the cache.\\n- Redux — when complex client state is needed (a cart, UI settings, forms, for example), or when the server and the client state are closely interwoven.\\n\\nConclusion: TanStack Query is the best choice for server state, Redux for client state. They can be combined in one project.","import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';\\n\\nfunction Posts() {\\n  const { data, isLoading, error } = useQuery({\\n    queryKey: ['posts'],\\n    queryFn: () => fetch('/api/posts').then(res => res.json()),\\n  });\\n\\n  if (isLoading) return 'Loading...';\\n  if (error) return 'Error!';\\n  return data.map(post => <div key={post.id}>{post.title}</div>);\\n}\\n\\n// actions.js\\nexport const fetchPosts = () => async (dispatch) => {\\n  dispatch({ type: 'posts/fetchStart' });\\n  try {\\n    const res = await fetch('/api/posts');\\n    const data = await res.json();\\n    dispatch({ type: 'posts/fetchSuccess', payload: data });\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    dispatch({ type: 'posts/fetchError', payload: err });\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// reducer.js\\nconst initialState = { data: [], isLoading: false, error: null };\\nexport default function postsReducer(state = initialState, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'posts/fetchStart': return { ...state, isLoading: true };\\n    case 'posts/fetchSuccess': return { ...state, isLoading: false, data: action.payload };\\n    case 'posts/fetchError': return { ...state, isLoading: false, error: action.payload };\\n    default: return state;\\n  }\\n}"],[2541,"To pass a parameter into an onClick without calling the function immediately, use an arrow function: onClick={() => handleClick(param)}. That creates a new function, which will be called on the click. Alternatively you can use .bind: onClick={handleClick.bind(null, param)}. A direct call handleClick(param) will run the function at once, during the render.","Passing parameters into an onClick handler\\nIn React and JavaScript it matters to pass parameters into event handlers in such a way that the function is not called immediately when the component renders. If you write \`onClick={handleClick(param)}\`, \`handleClick\` will run at once rather than on the click, which often leads to bugs.\\n\\nThe main ways\\n\\n- An arrow function: \`onClick={() => handleClick(param)}\` — it creates a new function, which will be called on the event. That is the most widespread and the most readable way.\\n- The .bind method: \`onClick={handleClick.bind(null, param)}\` — it returns a new function with the context and the arguments bound. It can be less performant with frequent re-renders, but it works properly.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example \`id\` is passed into \`handleClick\` only on the click rather than during the render.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse arrow functions for passing parameters into event handlers in React — that is simple, safe and in accordance with the best practices. Avoid calling functions directly in the JSX, in order to prevent the unexpected running of code.","function Button({ id }) {\\n  const handleClick = (param) => {\\n    console.log('Clicked:', param);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => handleClick(id)}>\\n      Click me\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}"],[2542,"React uses a shallow comparison in order to determine changes of state. When you call setState, React compares the previous and the new value by means of the strict equality operator (===). The mutation of an array through push changes the array's content, but the reference to the array stays the same. React therefore considers that the state has not changed and does not start a re-render.","Why does a mutation not cause a re-render?\\nIn React the state has to be immutable. When you use the \`useState\` hook, React holds a reference to the state object. When the update function (\`setState\`, for example) is called, React compares the new value with the previous one by means of \`Object.is\` (the analogue of \`===\`). If the reference has not changed, React considers that the state has stayed the same and skips the re-render.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nHow does this work under the hood?\\nReact uses the reconciliation algorithm. When \`setState\` is called, React marks the component as requiring an update. Then, during the render, it compares the previous virtual DOM tree with the new one. If the state has not changed (by reference), React skips the render of the component and of its child elements, which improves the performance.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAlways create new objects and arrays when updating state in React. Use the spread operator, \`map\`, \`filter\` or libraries such as Immer for convenient work with immutability. That guarantees the changes are detected properly and the interface behaves predictably.","const [items, setItems] = useState([1, 2, 3]);\\n\\n// Wrong: a mutation\\nitems.push(4);\\nsetItems(items); // React does not see the changes, so there will be no re-render\\n\\n// Right: the creation of a new array\\nsetItems([...items, 4]); // A new reference, so a re-render will happen"],[2543,"If an asynchronous operation in a useEffect returns an error, React does not handle it automatically. The error can lead to an unhandled promise, which will cause a warning in the console or a crash of the application. For proper handling you have to use try/catch inside the effect and update the error state. It also matters to cancel the requests when the component unmounts, through the cleanup function.","What happens on an error in an asynchronous useEffect?\\nWhen you perform an asynchronous operation inside a useEffect — a fetch request, for example — and it ends in an error, React does not intercept that error automatically. That can lead to an unhandled promise rejection, which in strict environments (in Node.js, for example) can terminate the process, and in the browser simply prints a warning to the console. The component, meanwhile, does not learn about the error, and the user sees no notification.\\n\\nHow do you handle the errors properly?\\nFor proper handling, use the try/catch construct inside the asynchronous function declared in the useEffect. Update the component's state, in order to display the error to the user. It also matters to cancel the requests when the component unmounts, so as to avoid an update of the state after the unmount (that causes a React warning).\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Always wrap your asynchronous code in a try/catch inside the useEffect.\\n- Use a flag or an AbortController in order to cancel the requests when it unmounts.\\n- Do not make the useEffect itself asynchronous — React expects the effect to return a cleanup function or undefined.\\n\\nConclusion: the handling of errors in a useEffect's asynchronous operations is an obligatory practice for stable React applications. It prevents unhandled promises and lets you notify the user of the problems properly.","useEffect(() => {\\n  let cancelled = false;\\n  async function fetchData() {\\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n      if (!cancelled) {\\n        setData(await response.json());\\n      }\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      if (!cancelled) {\\n        setError(error.message);\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  fetchData();\\n  return () => { cancelled = true; };\\n}, []);"],[2544,"Next.js is a framework based on React, which adds server-side rendering (SSR), static generation (SSG), routing based on the file system, and API routes. Unlike pure React, which is only a client-side library for the UI, Next.js provides a ready-made architecture for full-stack applications. It makes SEO easier, improves the performance through the preloading of data, and optimizes the bundles automatically.","The purpose of Next.js\\nNext.js is a production-ready framework for React, which solves tasks that go beyond a client-side library. It provides built-in solutions for server-side rendering (SSR), static generation (SSG), incremental static regeneration (ISR), routing, the optimization of images, and API routes. That lets you create both full web applications with server-side logic and highly performant static sites.\\n\\nThe key differences from pure React\\n\\n- Routing: in React, React Router is used, which works on the client. Next.js uses the file system for defining the routes (pages/ or app/), which makes the navigation easier and supports dynamic paths.\\n- Rendering: React renders everything on the client (CSR). Next.js lets you choose between CSR, SSR, SSG and ISR for every page, which improves the SEO and the loading time.\\n- API routes: Next.js lets you create server endpoints inside the project (pages/api), which removes the need for a separate backend for the simple tasks.\\n- Optimization: the built-in optimization of images, fonts and scripts, plus automatic code splitting — in React that requires additional libraries.\\n\\nAn example of the code: a page with SSR in Next.js\\n\\nIn this example the data is loaded on the server before the HTML is sent to the client, which improves the SEO and the time to the first content (the FCP). In pure React you would have to use a useEffect and handle the loading state.\\n\\nConclusion\\nNext.js is worth applying when SEO optimization, the fast loading of pages, built-in server-side logic or the static generation of content is required. For simple single-page applications without a need for SEO, pure React can be a lighter solution.","// pages/posts/[id].js\\nexport async function getServerSideProps(context) {\\n  const { id } = context.params;\\n  const res = await fetch(\`https://api.example.com/posts/\${id}\`);\\n  const post = await res.json();\\n  return { props: { post } };\\n}\\n\\nexport default function Post({ post }) {\\n  return <div><h1>{post.title}</h1><p>{post.body}</p></div>;\\n}"],[2545,"A React Fragment is React's built-in component, which lets you group several elements without adding a superfluous node to the DOM. It is used when several elements have to be returned from a component but a wrapper such as a \`<div>\` breaks the structure or the styles. A Fragment creates no real HTML element, which improves the performance and the cleanliness of the markup.","What is a React Fragment?\\nA React Fragment is a lightweight way of grouping child elements in JSX without adding a superfluous node to the DOM. In ordinary JSX you cannot return several elements without a wrapper, so a \`<div>\` is often used. That can lead to redundant nesting, to problems with CSS (Flexbox or Grid, for example) and to superfluous nodes that slow the rendering down, however.\\n\\nWhat is it for?\\nA Fragment solves those problems: it creates no real HTML element but only groups the children for React. That is especially useful in tables, in lists, or when using CSS grids, where a superfluous \`<div>\` can break the layout.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nYou can also use the shortened syntax \`<>...</>\`, which requires no import.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA React Fragment is a simple, effective tool for grouping elements without polluting the DOM. Apply it whenever a wrapper is needed only for the JSX rather than for the styles or the semantics.","import React, { Fragment } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Table() {\\n  return (\\n    <table>\\n      <tr>\\n        <Fragment>\\n          <td>Cell 1</td>\\n          <td>Cell 2</td>\\n        </Fragment>\\n      </tr>\\n    </table>\\n  );\\n}"],[2546,"useRef creates an object with a .current property, which is preserved between renders. For access to a DOM element you have to pass the ref into the element's attribute. useRef is also used for holding any mutable data (timers, previous values, for example), the changing of which does not cause a re-render of the component.","What useRef is, and how it works\\nuseRef is a React hook, which returns a mutable ref object with a single .current property. That object is preserved throughout the component's whole lifecycle and does not change between renders. Unlike useState, a change of ref.current does not cause a repeat render of the component.\\n\\nAccess to DOM elements\\nuseRef's main application is obtaining direct access to a DOM element. That is useful for focusing an input, for animations, and for integration with libraries that work with the DOM directly.\\n\\nHolding mutable values\\nuseRef is also used for holding any data that has to be preserved between renders without affecting them. For holding a timer's identifier, a prop's previous value or an instance of a third-party object, for example.\\n\\nConclusion\\nuseRef is a universal tool for working with the DOM and for holding mutable data in React. It is worth applying when you need direct access to an element or have to preserve a value without causing a re-render — for timers, animations or integration with external libraries, for example.","import { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction AutoFocusInput() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    inputRef.current.focus();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <input ref={inputRef} type=\\"text\\" />;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useRef, useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Timer() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const intervalRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n    return () => clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}"],[2547,"useContext is a React hook, which lets components obtain access to the data from a context without passing props through the whole chain. It solves the problem of prop drilling, where the data has to be passed through many levels of nested components. Instead you create a context by means of createContext, wrap the components in a Provider and use useContext in order to read the value.","What useContext is, and what it is for\\nuseContext is a hook in React, which lets a component subscribe to a context and obtain its current value. A context is a mechanism for passing data through the component tree without the need to pass props at every level. The main problem useContext solves is props drilling, where data is passed through a great many intermediate components that do not use it themselves. That makes the code cumbersome and hard to maintain.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nFirst a context is created by means of \`React.createContext()\`. Then a provider component wraps the child components and passes the value in through the \`value\` attribute. Any component inside the provider can use \`useContext(MyContext)\` in order to obtain that value. When the context's value changes, all of the subscribed components re-render.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen to apply it?\\nuseContext is ideal for global data that changes rarely, such as the theme, the language or the authentication. For state that updates often (a form's data, for example), however, it is better to use Redux or other libraries, since a context can cause redundant re-renders. Conclusion: useContext is a simple, effective way of avoiding prop drilling, but it is worth applying for data that does not change too often.","import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext('light');\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Current theme: {theme}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>\\n        Toggle\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2548,"React uses a shallow comparison (===) for every element of the array of dependencies. If at least one value has changed by reference, the effect runs afresh. That lets React determine efficiently when the side effects have to be updated, avoiding redundant calls.","How the tracking of the dependencies in useEffect works\\nReact compares the dependencies' current values with the previous ones on every render. For that, strict equality (Object.is) is used, which is analogous to the === operator but takes NaN into account. If at least one dependency has changed, the effect is started again.\\n\\nAn example with primitives and objects\\n\\nFor primitives (numbers, strings) the comparison happens by value. For objects and arrays, by reference. A change of an object's property without the creation of a new object will therefore not cause the effect to run.\\n\\nThe practical application\\nThis mechanism is critical for optimizing the performance. If you pass into the dependencies an array that is created afresh on every render, the effect will run every time. To avoid that, useMemo or useCallback is used, in order to stabilize the references.\\n\\nConclusion: an understanding of the shallow comparison of the dependencies helps you avoid infinite loops and redundant calls of the effects, which matters especially in complex React applications.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\nconst [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alice' });\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  console.log('The effect has run');\\n}, [count, user]);\\n\\n// On setCount(1) — the effect will run (count has changed)\\n// On setUser({ name: 'Alice' }) — the effect will run (a new reference to the object)"],[2549,"A call of a hook after a conditional return breaks React's rules of hooks. React relies on the order in which the hooks are called between renders. If a hook is called after a return, it can be skipped on the next render, which will lead to the error \\"Rendered fewer hooks than expected\\".","Why this is a problem\\nReact uses the order in which the hooks are called in order to match the state between renders. If a hook is called after a conditional return, then under different conditions of the render the number and the order of the hooks can change. That breaks React's internal expectations and causes an error.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nIn this example, if \`flag\` equals \`true\`, the component returns \`null\` and the hooks are not called. On the next render with \`flag = false\`, React expects the hooks to be called in the same order, but their number has changed, which leads to an error.\\n\\nHow to fix it\\nAll of the hooks have to be called at the top level of the component, before any conditional returns. A conditional return can be placed after all of the hooks, or conditional rendering can be used inside the JSX.\\n\\nConclusion\\nObserving the rules of hooks guarantees the components work stably and predictably. Always place your hooks before any conditional statements, in order to avoid runtime errors.","function MyComponent({ flag }) {\\n  if (flag) {\\n    return null;\\n  }\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(0); // An error!\\n  useEffect(() => {}, []);\\n  return <div>{value}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent({ flag }) {\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(0);\\n  useEffect(() => {}, []);\\n  if (flag) {\\n    return null;\\n  }\\n  return <div>{value}</div>;\\n}"],[2550,"Micro-frontends can interact through a global event mechanism (an Event Bus), shared state (a Redux store, for example), URL parameters, or through the parent container. Every micro-frontend subscribes to the events or reads the shared data. That lets them exchange information without breaking the isolation.","The ways in which micro-frontends interact\\nMicro-frontends are independent parts of an interface, which are developed and deployed separately but work together in one application. To coordinate them, mechanisms of communication are needed that do not break their autonomy.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- An Event Bus: a global object, through which the micro-frontends send and receive events. One micro-frontend publishes a 'userLoggedIn' event, for example, while another subscribes to it and updates the UI.\\n- Shared State: the use of a single store, such as Redux or MobX, available to all of the micro-frontends. Changes in one module are reflected in the others automatically.\\n- The URL and the routing: the passing of data through the query parameters or the hash of the URL. When moving between micro-frontends, for example, the information about the current user is passed in the address bar.\\n- The parent container (the shell): the main application acts as the intermediary, passing props in or calling the child modules' methods.\\n\\nAn example with an Event Bus\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of method depends on the application's complexity: for the simple scenarios an Event Bus will do, for the complex ones shared state. The main thing is to preserve loose coupling, so that the micro-frontends stay independent.","// Micro-frontend A publishes an event\\nwindow.eventBus.publish('cartUpdated', { items: 3 });\\n\\n// Micro-frontend B subscribes\\nwindow.eventBus.subscribe('cartUpdated', (data) => {\\n  console.log('The cart has been updated:', data.items);\\n});"],[2551,"The Context API suits the simple passing of data that changes rarely — the theme or the language, for example. An external state manager (Redux, Zustand) is needed with frequent updates, complex logic or a large number of interrelated pieces of state. Context causes a re-render of all of the consumers on any change, which reduces the performance. The external solutions optimize the updates and provide tools for debugging.","When the Context API is sufficient\\nThe Context API in React is intended for passing data through the component tree without an explicit drilling of props. It works well for static or rarely changing values, such as the design theme, the user's locale or the authentication data. In those scenarios the updates happen infrequently and the performance does not suffer.\\n\\nWhen an external state manager is needed\\nIf the state updates often (on every press of a key in a form, for example) or has complex logic with a great many dependencies, Context can cause excessive re-renders. External libraries such as Redux or Zustand use subscriptions to individual parts of the state, which minimizes the repaints. They also provide middleware, devtools and a strict architecture for large applications.\\n\\nAn example of a comparison\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the Context API for global data that changes rarely. For dynamic state with frequent updates or complex business logic, choose an external state manager, in order to preserve the performance and the maintainability of the code.","// Context - a re-render of all of the consumers on a change\\nconst ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Header /> {/* it re-renders */}\\n      <Footer /> {/* it re-renders */}\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Redux - only the subscribed component updates\\nconst selectTheme = (state) => state.theme;\\nfunction Header() {\\n  const theme = useSelector(selectTheme);\\n  return <div>{theme}</div>;\\n}"],[2552,"Yes, React guarantees that the reference to the setter function returned from useState stays stable between renders. That means the function is not recreated on every render, unless the component unmounts and mounts afresh. Such stability lets you use the setter in effects' dependencies safely, or pass it into child components without redundant re-renders.","The stability of the reference to the setter from useState\\nReact guarantees that the setter function returned from the useState hook preserves one and the same reference between the component's renders. That means that on every call of useState in one and the same component, one and the same function for updating the state is returned, unless the component has been unmounted completely and mounted afresh.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nThe stability of the reference is critical for optimizing the performance in React. When you pass the setter as a prop to a child component or use it in the array of dependencies of a useEffect hook, a stable reference prevents unnecessary re-renders and repeat calls of the effects. If the reference changed every render, that would lead to infinite loops or redundant computation.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example setCount is passed into the useEffect's dependencies. Thanks to the stability of the reference, the effect runs only when the component mounts rather than on every change of count.\\n\\nThe exceptions and the particulars\\n\\n- The stability is guaranteed only for the setter from useState. Other hooks, useReducer for example, also return a stable dispatch.\\n- If the component unmounts and mounts afresh (on a change of the key, for example), the setter will be a new one.\\n- In React's strict mode (StrictMode) the renders can be called twice, but the reference to the setter stays stable within one mounting.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe stability of the reference to the setter from useState is a fundamental feature of React, which makes optimization easier and prevents bugs related to changing references. Use that property for passing the setter into child components or into effects' dependencies safely, without fear of redundant re-renders.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // setCount is stable, so the effect will run only once\\n    console.log('Effect runs');\\n  }, [setCount]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2553,"When a timer updates the state in the parent component, React repaints the parent and all of its child components, even if their props have not changed. That happens because new references to functions and objects are created on every render. It can be fixed by means of React.memo for memoizing the child component, useCallback for stabilizing the function props and useMemo for stabilizing the objects.","Why a timer causes a re-render of the child components\\nIn React, every time a component's state updates (through a setInterval, for example), a re-render of it happens. During a re-render of the parent, all of the child components also repaint by default, even if their props have not changed. That is because React does not compare the props automatically — it simply calls render for all of the descendants.\\n\\nThe problem is aggravated if functions or objects created directly in the JSX are passed into the props. New references are created on every render, which breaks even React.memo, unless useCallback or useMemo is used.\\n\\nHow to fix it\\nThere are three main approaches:\\n- React.memo — it wraps a component, so that it repaints only when the props change (a shallow comparison).\\n- useCallback — it memoizes a function, so that its reference stays stable between renders.\\n- useMemo — it memoizes the result of a computation or an object.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example Child is wrapped in React.memo, and handleClick is stabilized through a useCallback. Thanks to that, Child will not repaint on every tick of the timer.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse React.memo for components that often receive the same props, and useCallback/useMemo for stabilizing the references. That matters especially when working with timers, animations or large lists, in order to avoid redundant re-renders and preserve the application's performance.","import React, { useState, useEffect, useCallback, memo } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Child = memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child re-render');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const timer = setInterval(() => setCount(c => c + 1), 1000);\\n    return () => clearInterval(timer);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  // Without a useCallback — a new function every render\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    console.log('Clicked');\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <Child onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2554,"The main ways: React.memo for memoizing a component, useMemo for memoizing values, useCallback for memoizing callback functions. You can also move heavy computations into a useMemo and avoid passing new objects and functions on every render. That helps React skip the render if the props have not changed.","The problem of redundant re-renders\\nIn React, when a parent component's state updates, all of its child components re-render by default, even if their props have not changed. That can lead to reduced performance, especially in deep trees of components or with heavy computations.\\n\\nThe main ways of preventing it\\n\\n- React.memo — it wraps a component and compares the props shallowly. If the props have not changed, the component does not re-render.\\n- useMemo — it memoizes the result of a computation, so that objects and arrays are not recreated on every render.\\n- useCallback — it memoizes a function, so that the reference to it stays stable between renders.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example \`Child\` is wrapped in \`React.memo\`, and the \`data\` and \`onClick\` props are stable, thanks to the \`useMemo\` and the \`useCallback\`. When \`other\` changes, \`Child\` will not re-render.\\n\\nAdditional techniques\\n\\n- Lifting state up — moving the shared state higher, in order to avoid redundant renders.\\n- The use of keys for the proper comparison of the elements of a list.\\n- The separation of components into containers and presentational ones.\\n\\nConclusion: apply \`React.memo\`, \`useMemo\` and \`useCallback\` deliberately, only when profiling shows a performance problem. Excessive memoization can complicate the code and give no gain.","import React, { useState, useMemo, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ onClick, data }) => {\\n  console.log('Child render');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>{data.value}</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const [other, setOther] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // A useMemo for a stable object\\n  const data = useMemo(() => ({ value: count }), [count]);\\n\\n  // A useCallback for a stable function\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Child onClick={handleClick} data={data} />\\n      <button onClick={() => setOther(o => o + 1)}>Other: {other}</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2555,"The linter (eslint-plugin-react-hooks) requires you to state every variable and function used inside the useEffect in the array of dependencies. An empty array means the effect runs only once, on mounting. If props, state or other values are used inside the effect, they can be stale (a stale closure). The linter warns about that in order to prevent bugs. If the empty array is deliberate, you have to either move the logic into a useRef or add the comment // eslint-disable-next-line.","Why does the linter complain about an empty array of dependencies in a useEffect?\\nThe linter — specifically the \`eslint-plugin-react-hooks\` plugin — analyses the code for compliance with React's rules of hooks. One of the key rules is \`react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\`. It requires that every variable, function and prop used inside a \`useEffect\` be listed in the array of dependencies. If the array is empty while there are references to external values inside the effect, the linter issues a warning or an error.\\n\\nThe problem of stale closures\\nWhen you pass an empty array \`[]\`, the effect runs only once — when the component mounts. All of the variables captured in the effect's closure preserve their values as of the moment of the first render. If those variables change later (through \`useState\` or props, for example), the effect will keep using the old values. That is called a stale closure.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nIn this example \`count\` inside the \`setInterval\` will always equal 0, because the effect captured it on the first render. The linter warns that \`count\` should be in the dependencies.\\n\\nWhen an empty array is deliberate\\nSometimes an empty array really is needed — for a subscription to an external source of data, or for analytics that has to fire once, for example. In such cases you can:\\n- Use a \`useRef\` for holding the current values, in order to avoid stale closures.\\n- Add the comment \`// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\` above the line with the array, in order to switch the warning off.\\n\\nThe right approach\\n\\nIn this version we use the functional form of \`setCount\`, which does not depend on the external \`count\`. The linter sees that there are no references to variables from the render inside the effect, so an empty array is permissible.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe linter complains about an empty array of dependencies in order to prevent bugs with stale closures. If the empty array is deliberate, make sure no external variables are used inside the effect, or use a \`useRef\` for holding them. As a last resort you can switch the rule off for the particular line, but that should be a deliberate decision.","function Timer({ start }) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const id = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(count + 1); // count is always 0, since it was captured on mounting\\n    }, 1000);\\n    return () => clearInterval(id);\\n  }, []); // the linter complains: count is not stated in the dependencies\\n\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Timer({ start }) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const id = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1); // we use the functional form of setState\\n    }, 1000);\\n    return () => clearInterval(id);\\n  }, []); // now the linter does not complain, since there are no external dependencies\\n\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}"],[2556,"You cannot pass an asynchronous function into a useEffect directly, since it returns a promise rather than a clean-up function. Instead you have to declare an asynchronous function inside the callback and call it. It also matters to handle the errors with a try/catch and to use the clean-up function in order to cancel the requests when the component unmounts.","The problem with async/await in a useEffect\\nThe useEffect hook expects the function it is given either to return nothing or to return a clean-up function. An asynchronous function always returns a promise, so passing it in directly will cause an error or undesirable behaviour.\\n\\nThe right approach\\nCreate an asynchronous function inside the useEffect's callback and call it at once. That lets you use await and handle the errors.\\n\\nHandling the cancellation of requests\\nTo prevent memory leaks when the component unmounts, use a flag or an AbortController.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse an inner asynchronous function with a try/catch and a clean-up function, for working with asynchronous operations in a useEffect safely. That prevents memory leaks and handles the errors properly.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const fetchData = async () => {\\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n      const data = await response.json();\\n      setData(data);\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      console.error('A loading error:', error);\\n    }\\n  };\\n  fetchData();\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const controller = new AbortController();\\n  const fetchData = async () => {\\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal });\\n      const data = await response.json();\\n      setData(data);\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {\\n        console.error(error);\\n      }\\n    }\\n  };\\n  fetchData();\\n  return () => controller.abort();\\n}, []);"],[2557,"useEffect takes a synchronous function so that React can guarantee the effect runs after the render while the clean-up function is called before the unmount or a repeat run. The synchrony lets React manage the order of execution and avoid races in the data. If the function were asynchronous, React would not be able to handle the clean-up and the dependency on the previous state properly.","Why does useEffect take a synchronous function?\\nuseEffect in React is intended for performing side effects such as requests to an API, subscriptions or manipulations of the DOM. The first argument has to be a synchronous function, so that React can control its running and guarantee the effect is started only once the render has completed. That also lets React call the clean-up function returned from the effect at the right moment — before the component unmounts, or before the effect is run again when the dependencies change.\\n\\nHow does this work in practice?\\nWhen you pass an asynchronous function in directly, React cannot wait for it to complete, which can lead to problems with the clean-up and to memory leaks. Instead you should declare an asynchronous function inside the synchronous callback and call it. For example:\\n\\nHere the outer function is synchronous, which lets React manage its running and the clean-up properly.\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\nThis approach is used in every React application for working with side effects. It guarantees the components behave predictably and prevents errors related to asynchrony.\\n\\nConclusion: use a synchronous function in a useEffect, so that React can manage the effect's lifecycle properly, and perform the asynchronous operations inside it, for safety and cleanliness of the code.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const fetchData = async () => {\\n    const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    setData(data);\\n  };\\n  fetchData();\\n}, []);"],[2558,"When a state-update function is called in a child component, React updates the state in the parent component. That causes a repeat render of the parent component and of all of its child components, including the one that called the function. React re-renders all of the child elements by default, unless optimizations such as React.memo are used.","How the rendering works when a state-update function is passed\\nIn React the state is managed at the level of the component where it is declared by means of the useState hook. When a state-update function (setState) is passed into a child component through props and called there, it changes the state in the parent component specifically. That starts the process of repeat rendering, which begins with the parent and spreads down the component tree.\\n\\nWhich components re-render\\n\\n- The parent component, where the state is declared, re-renders first.\\n- All of the parent's child components, including the one that called the function, also re-render by default.\\n- If the child components do not use React.memo or other optimizations, they will be re-rendered independently of whether their props have changed.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nOn a press of the button in Child, onIncrement is called, which updates count in Parent. That leads to a repeat render of Parent and of Child, as can be seen from the message in the console.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of this mechanism matters for optimizing the performance of React applications. If a child component should not re-render on every change of the parent's state, use React.memo or extract the state into a separate context.","function Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <Child onIncrement={() => setCount(c => c + 1)} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Child({ onIncrement }) {\\n  console.log('Child re-rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onIncrement}>Increment</button>;\\n}"],[2559,"Redux uses a unidirectional data flow. When the user initiates an action, dispatch(action) is called. Redux passes the action and the current state into the reducer, which returns the new state. Then the store is updated and the subscribed components repaint.","How the Redux cycle works\\nRedux is a predictable state container for JavaScript applications. Its key feature is the unidirectional data flow, which makes the changes of state predictable and easy to track.\\n\\nAn action's path from the dispatch to the update of the state\\n\\n- The action: the user or the code initiates an action, creating an action object with a type field and optional data (a payload).\\n- dispatch(action): the call of store.dispatch(action) sends the action to the store.\\n- The reducer: the store passes the current state and the action into the root reducer. A reducer is a pure function, which takes the previous state and the action and returns the new state without mutating the original.\\n- The update of the store: the store replaces the current state with the new one returned by the reducer.\\n- The notification of the subscribers: the store calls all of the subscribed listeners (through connect or useSelector in React, for example), which leads to the components repainting.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Redux cycle provides a strict sequence: action → dispatch → reducer → the new state → the update of the UI. That makes debugging, testing and the maintenance of large applications easier, where it matters to control the changes of state.","// An action creator\\nconst increment = () => ({ type: 'INCREMENT' });\\n\\n// A reducer\\nconst counterReducer = (state = { count: 0 }, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return { count: state.count + 1 };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// The store\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\n\\n// The dispatch\\nstore.dispatch(increment());\\nconsole.log(store.getState()); // { count: 1 }"],[2560,"createListenerMiddleware is a middleware built into Redux Toolkit, which lets you start side effects in response to Redux actions. It listens for particular actions and runs callbacks, which is convenient for logging, navigation or API calls. Unlike createAsyncThunk, it creates no new actions but simply responds to the existing ones.","What is createListenerMiddleware?\\ncreateListenerMiddleware is a middleware from Redux Toolkit, which lets you run arbitrary code (side effects) in response to the dispatch of particular actions. It resembles sagas or epics, but it is simpler and lighter to use, since it requires no additional libraries.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nThe middleware intercepts every dispatch and checks whether it matches the conditions set (the action's type, for example). If the condition is met, a callback is started, which can perform asynchronous operations, dispatch other actions or interact with the state.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Logging the user's actions\\n- Navigation after a successful action (a redirect after a login, for example)\\n- Synchronization with localStorage or IndexedDB\\n- An API call without creating a thunk\\n\\nConclusion\\ncreateListenerMiddleware is a convenient tool for handling side effects in Redux, when the full power of sagas or thunks is not required. It is especially useful for the simple scenarios, where you have to respond to actions without changing the reducers' logic.","import { createListenerMiddleware, isAnyOf } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nimport { addTodo, removeTodo } from './todosSlice';\\n\\nconst listenerMiddleware = createListenerMiddleware();\\n\\nlistenerMiddleware.startListening({\\n  matcher: isAnyOf(addTodo, removeTodo),\\n  effect: async (action, listenerApi) => {\\n    console.log('A todo has been changed:', action);\\n    // Other actions can be dispatched\\n    listenerApi.dispatch({ type: 'log/action', payload: action.type });\\n  },\\n});\\n\\nexport default listenerMiddleware;"],[2561,"React.memo is a higher-order component, which memoizes the result of a component's rendering, preventing a repeat render of it when the props are unchanged. useMemo is a hook, which memoizes the result of a function's computation, preventing expensive computations on every render. React.memo works at the level of the component, useMemo at the level of the value.","The main difference\\nReact.memo and useMemo serve to optimize the performance, but they work at different levels. React.memo is a higher-order component (HOC), which wraps a component and prevents a repeat render of it if the props have not changed (a shallow comparison). useMemo is a hook, which memoizes the result of running a function and recomputes it only when the dependencies change.\\n\\nExamples of the usage\\nReact.memo is applied to a component:\\n\\nuseMemo is used inside a functional component:\\n\\nWhen to apply which\\n\\n- React.memo — when a repeat render of a whole component has to be avoided while the props are unchanged.\\n- useMemo — when expensive computations have to be avoided, or when the referential stability of an object or an array has to be preserved between renders.\\n\\nConclusion: React.memo optimizes a component's render, while useMemo optimizes computations. Use them together for maximum performance, but do not abuse them, since memoization also requires resources.","const MyComponent = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  console.log('Rendered');\\n  return <div>{data}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nconst sortedList = useMemo(() => {\\n  return items.sort((a, b) => a - b);\\n}, [items]);"],[2562,"A functional component re-renders when the setter function returned by useState is called. React compares the new value with the current one by means of Object.is. If the values are different, the component and its child elements re-render. If they are the same, no re-render happens.","When a re-render happens when useState is used\\nIn React a functional component re-renders every time the setter function obtained from the \`useState\` hook is called. That is the main mechanism of updating the UI in response to changes of state.\\n\\nThe comparison mechanism\\nReact uses the strict comparison \`Object.is\` in order to determine whether the state's value has changed. If the new value differs from the previous one, the component re-renders. If the values are the same (when the same object or primitive is passed in, for example), no re-render happens.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe important nuances\\n\\n- When a function is passed into the setter (\`setCount(prev => prev + 1)\`, for example), React guarantees the current value of the state will be used.\\n- If the state is an object or an array, a new reference has to be created for a re-render to happen. A mutation of the existing object will not cause a re-render.\\n- A re-render of a component can also be caused by a change of the props or of the context.\\n\\nConclusion: use useState for a component's local state. Remember that a re-render happens only when the value changes rather than on every call of the setter. For complex state, use the functional form of the update.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1); // a re-render will happen\\n  };\\n\\n  const handleSameValue = () => {\\n    setCount(count); // a re-render will NOT happen, since the value has not changed\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>Increment</button>\\n      <button onClick={handleSameValue}>Set same</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2563,"useTransition is a React hook, which lets you mark a state update as low-priority. It helps you avoid blocking the interface while performing heavy operations, such as the rendering of large lists. The hook returns a tuple: isPending (a waiting flag) and startTransition (the function for starting the transition). That improves the user experience by making the application more responsive.","What is useTransition?\\nuseTransition is a hook from React 18, which is part of Concurrent Mode. It lets the developer state that a particular state update is less of a priority and can be deferred, so as not to block the user's more urgent interactions, such as typing text or clicking.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen you call startTransition, React marks all of the state updates inside it as transitional. React can interrupt those updates if a higher-priority task appears (an update from the input, for example). That prevents the interface hanging.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nWhere to apply it?\\n\\n- A search with the filtering of large data\\n- Switching between tabs with heavy content\\n- Animations and transitions between pages\\n\\nConclusion: useTransition is useful when the responsiveness of the interface has to be preserved while resource-intensive operations are performed, especially in applications with frequent state updates.","import { useState, useTransition } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction SearchComponent() {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\\n  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);\\n  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();\\n\\n  const handleChange = (e) => {\\n    setQuery(e.target.value); // the urgent update\\n    startTransition(() => {\\n      // the low-priority update\\n      setResults(filterData(e.target.value));\\n    });\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={query} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      {isPending && <span>Loading...</span>}\\n      <ul>{results.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2564,"On every render a new array object with a new reference in memory is created. If the array is passed as a prop to a child component, React.memo will not be able to prevent the re-render, since the comparison by reference will show a change. That leads to redundant re-renders and reduced performance.","The problem of creating a new array on every render\\nIn React, on every render of a component all of the variables declared inside it are created afresh. That applies to arrays and objects too. Even if the array's content has not changed, a new object with a new reference in memory is created.\\n\\nThe effect on the child components\\nIf such an array is passed as a prop to a child component wrapped in React.memo, the memoization will not fire. React.memo compares the props shallowly — that is, by reference. A new reference will be taken as a change, and the child component will re-render even if the data has not changed.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen this is critical\\nThe problem becomes noticeable with a large number of child components, with complex computations inside them, or with frequent re-renders of the parent. In simple applications it may be unnoticeable, but in large projects redundant re-renders can slow things down substantially.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse useMemo for stable arrays and objects that are passed as props into memoized components. That prevents redundant re-renders and improves the application's performance.","// Bad: a new array on every render\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const items = ['a', 'b', 'c']; // a new reference every time\\n  return <Child list={items} />;\\n}\\n\\n// Good: the memoization of the array\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const items = useMemo(() => ['a', 'b', 'c'], []);\\n  return <Child list={items} />;\\n}"],[2565,"useMemo caches the result of a computation between renders. If the dependencies have not changed, the saved value is returned. That prevents arrays and objects being recreated on every render, which optimizes the performance.","What useMemo is, and what it is for\\nuseMemo is a React hook, which lets you memoize (cache) the result of a computation between a component's renders. When a component repaints, all of the variables and functions inside it are created afresh. For arrays and objects that means a new instance is created every time, even if the content has not changed. That can lead to redundant re-renders of the child components that depend on that data.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nuseMemo takes two arguments: a function returning a value, and an array of dependencies. React remembers the function's result and returns it on the repeat renders, if the dependencies have stayed the same. If the dependencies have changed, the function runs afresh.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen to apply useMemo?\\nUse useMemo for expensive computations (the filtering of large arrays, complex mathematical operations, for example) or for stabilizing the references to objects and arrays that are passed into child components wrapped in React.memo. You should not memoize everything indiscriminately, however — that adds the overhead of comparing the dependencies. Apply it only when there is a real performance problem.\\n\\nConclusion: useMemo helps you avoid redundant computations and re-renders by caching the result until the dependencies change. That is useful for optimizing React applications, especially when working with large data or with components that repaint often.","import React, { useMemo } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent({ items, filter }) {\\n  // Without a useMemo: every render creates a new array\\n  // const filteredItems = items.filter(item => item.includes(filter));\\n\\n  // With a useMemo: the array is recreated only when items or filter changes\\n  const filteredItems = useMemo(() => {\\n    return items.filter(item => item.includes(filter));\\n  }, [items, filter]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {filteredItems.map(item => <li key={item}>{item}</li>)}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2566,"React.memo does not guarantee the absence of redundant re-renders when complex objects or arrays are passed in. It uses a shallow comparison of the props — that is, it compares the references rather than the content. If a new object or array is created on every render of the parent, the reference changes and React.memo considers the props changed, causing a re-render of the child component.","How React.memo works\\nReact.memo is a higher-order component, which remembers the result of the rendering and skips a repeat render if the props have not changed. By default it uses a shallow comparison — it compares the references to the objects rather than their content.\\n\\nThe problem with objects and arrays\\nIf complex objects or arrays are passed into the props that are created afresh on every render of the parent (object or array literals, the results of calls of functions), then the reference to them will be new. React.memo will see that the reference has changed and will perform a re-render, even if the object's or the array's content has stayed the same.\\n\\nIn this example Child will re-render on every change of count, despite the React.memo, because the data object is created afresh.\\n\\nHow to solve the problem\\nTo avoid redundant re-renders, you have to stabilize the references to the objects and the arrays. For that you can use:\\n- \`useMemo\` — for memoizing computed values\\n- \`useCallback\` — for memoizing functions\\n- Moving the constants outside the component\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact.memo is effective only with stable references in the props. For complex objects and arrays you have to use memoization by means of useMemo, or move the immutable data outside the component. Otherwise React.memo will not give the effect expected and can even make the performance worse, because of the overhead of the comparison.","// An example: a new object is created every time\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const data = { name: 'John' }; // a new reference on every render\\n  return <Child data={data} />;\\n}\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  console.log('Child re-rendered');\\n  return <div>{data.name}</div>;\\n});\\n\\n// The use of a useMemo for stabilizing the reference\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const data = useMemo(() => ({ name: 'John' }), []);\\n  return <Child data={data} />;\\n}"],[2567,"A call of setState inside a component marks it as requiring an update. React then re-renders that component and all of its child components by default, even if their props have not changed. That can lead to unnecessary re-renders, so React.memo or shouldComponentUpdate is used for optimization.","How setState affects the child components\\nWhen you call setState in a parent component, React marks that component as 'dirty' and schedules a re-render of it. During the next render React creates a new virtual DOM tree for the parent and all of its child elements. By default the child components also re-render, even if their props have stayed the same.\\n\\nWhy this happens\\nReact does not compare the props automatically — it simply calls the render function for every child component. That guarantees the UI is always synchronized with the state, but it can be redundant. For example:\\n\\nOn every click Child will print the message, even though its content does not change.\\n\\nHow to optimize it\\n\\n- React.memo — it wraps a component and prevents a re-render if the props have not changed (a shallow comparison).\\n- useMemo/useCallback — they memoize values and functions, so that the props stay stable.\\n- shouldComponentUpdate (in class components) — it lets you manage the re-render by hand.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of this mechanism matters for writing performant React applications. Use React.memo for components that often re-render without their props changing, and avoid passing new objects or functions on every render unnecessarily.","function Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click</button>\\n      <Child />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Child() {\\n  console.log('Child re-rendered');\\n  return <div>Static content</div>;\\n}"],[2568,"If the child components are not wrapped in React.memo, then on every re-render of the parent they will re-render, independently of whether their props have changed. React re-renders all of the child components recursively by default. React.memo prevents that by performing a shallow comparison of the props.","The behaviour of the child components on a re-render of the parent\\nIn React, when a parent component re-renders (because of a change of state or props, for example), all of its child components also re-render by default. That happens recursively: every child component in turn re-renders its own descendants. Such behaviour guarantees that the user interface is always synchronized with the application's current state.\\n\\nAn example without React.memo\\n\\nOn every click on the button both child components will re-render, even if their props have not changed. That can be seen from the output in the console.\\n\\nHow React.memo solves the problem\\nReact.memo is a higher-order component, which remembers the result of the rendering. It performs a shallow comparison of the props and, if the props have not changed, uses the saved result, preventing an unnecessary re-render.\\n\\nNow, when the parent's state changes, the child components will not re-render if their props have stayed the same.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse React.memo for optimizing the performance in cases where the child components often re-render because of changes in the parent while their props change rarely. You should not wrap every component in React.memo unnecessarily, however, since that adds the overhead of comparing the props.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Child = ({ name }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered:', name);\\n  return <div>{name}</div>;\\n};\\n\\nconst Parent = () => {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Click</button>\\n      <Child name=\\"Alice\\" />\\n      <Child name=\\"Bob\\" />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ name }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered:', name);\\n  return <div>{name}</div>;\\n});"],[2569,"On the first mounting React renders the parent component first, then the children. Once the rendering of all of the components in the tree has completed, React starts the effects (useEffect) synchronously, in the order from the children to the parents. That guarantees the DOM has already been updated before the side effects run.","The order of the rendering of nested React components on the first mounting\\nReact uses a recursive approach for rendering the component tree. On the first mounting the process is divided into two phases: the render phase and the commit phase.\\n\\nThe render phase\\nReact begins with the root component and calls its render function. If the component returns child components, React renders them recursively before finishing the parent's render. Thus the order in which the render functions are called goes from the top down: first the parent, then its children, then the grandchildren, and so on.\\n\\nThe commit phase and the effects\\nOnce the rendering of all of the components has completed, React updates the real DOM. Then the effects (useEffect) are started. The effects run in the order from the child components to the parents (from the bottom up). That is because the children's effects may depend on the DOM created by the parent.\\n\\nThe practical application\\nThis order matters for working with side effects properly, such as subscriptions to events or data requests. If a child component uses data obtained in the parent's effect, for example, that can lead to errors, since the parent's effect runs later.\\n\\nConclusion: an understanding of the order of the rendering and of the effects helps you avoid bugs when working with nested components and with dependencies in a useEffect. Always take into account that the rendering goes from the top down while the effects go from the bottom up.","function Parent() {\\n  console.log('Parent render');\\n  return <Child />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Child() {\\n  console.log('Child render');\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// The output: Parent render, Child render\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  useEffect(() => console.log('Parent effect'));\\n  return <Child />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Child() {\\n  useEffect(() => console.log('Child effect'));\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// The output: Child effect, Parent effect"],[2570,"useEffect can imitate componentDidMount with an empty array of dependencies, componentDidUpdate when dependencies are stated, and componentWillUnmount through the clean-up function. For componentDidMount pass an empty array; for componentDidUpdate an array with the variables being tracked; for componentWillUnmount return a function from the useEffect.","The correspondence of useEffect to the lifecycle methods\\nThe useEffect hook in React unites the functionality of several lifecycle methods of class components. It lets you perform side effects after the rendering of a component and manage their clean-up.\\n\\ncomponentDidMount\\nTo reproduce the behaviour of componentDidMount (running code once after the component mounts), a useEffect with an empty array of dependencies is used:\\n\\ncomponentDidUpdate\\nTo imitate componentDidUpdate (running code when particular props or state change), state the dependencies in the array:\\n\\nIf the effect has to run on any update, omit the array of dependencies (but that can lead to an infinite loop).\\n\\ncomponentWillUnmount\\nTo clean the resources up when the component unmounts (the analogue of componentWillUnmount), return a function from the useEffect:\\n\\nThe clean-up function also runs before the effect is started again, if the dependencies have changed.\\n\\nConclusion\\nuseEffect is a universal tool for managing side effects in functional React components. It replaces componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate and componentWillUnmount, making the code more concise and more predictable. Use it for working with APIs, subscriptions, timers and other asynchronous operations.","useEffect(() => {\\n  // The code runs once after the mounting\\n  fetchData();\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  // The code runs on every change of userId\\n  fetchUserData(userId);\\n}, [userId]);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const subscription = subscribeToUpdates();\\n  return () => {\\n    // The clean-up on the unmounting\\n    subscription.unsubscribe();\\n  };\\n}, []);"],[2571,"The lifecycle methods in React class components are special functions, which are called automatically at the different stages of a component's existence: the mounting, the updating and the unmounting. The main methods: constructor(), render(), componentDidMount(), componentDidUpdate(), componentWillUnmount(). They let you perform the initialization, the updating of the data and the clean-up of the resources.","What are the lifecycle methods?\\nIn React class components a component's lifecycle is divided into three phases: the mounting (the creation and insertion into the DOM), the updating (when props or state change) and the unmounting (the removal from the DOM). For every phase there are special methods, which React calls automatically. They give the developer control over the component's behaviour at the key moments.\\n\\nThe main methods and their application\\n\\n- constructor(props) — it is called when the component is created. It is used for initializing the state and binding the methods. setState() cannot be called in it.\\n- render() — the obligatory method; it returns JSX. It has to be a pure function without side effects.\\n- componentDidMount() — it is called immediately after the component mounts into the DOM. It is ideal for requests to an API, for subscriptions and for setting timers up.\\n- componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState) — it is called after the component updates. It lets you respond to changes of props or state — for a repeat request of the data, for example.\\n- componentWillUnmount() — it is called before the component is removed from the DOM. It is used for the clean-up: unsubscribing from events, stopping timers, cancelling requests.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe lifecycle methods in React class components provide a structured way of managing side effects and state. They are especially useful in complex components, where precise control over the initialization, the updating and the clean-up of the resources is required. In modern development with functional components and hooks (useEffect), however, these methods are used more rarely, but an understanding of how they work remains important for maintaining legacy code and for a deep understanding of React.","class Timer extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    this.state = { seconds: 0 };\\n  }\\n\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    this.interval = setInterval(() => {\\n      this.setState({ seconds: this.state.seconds + 1 });\\n    }, 1000);\\n  }\\n\\n  componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState) {\\n    if (this.state.seconds === 10) {\\n      console.log('10 seconds reached');\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  componentWillUnmount() {\\n    clearInterval(this.interval);\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>Seconds: {this.state.seconds}</div>;\\n  }\\n}"],[2572,"These are lifecycle methods of React class components. componentDidMount is called after the component mounts and is used for requests to an API and for subscriptions. componentDidUpdate is called after the props or the state update and lets you respond to the changes. componentWillUnmount is called before the component is removed and is used for cleaning subscriptions and timers up. componentDidCatch catches errors in the child components and lets you display a fallback UI.","The lifecycle methods of React class components\\nIn React class components there are special methods, which are called automatically at the different stages of a component's life: the mounting, the updating, the unmounting and the handling of errors. These methods let the developer perform side effects such as requests to a server, subscriptions or the clean-up of resources.\\n\\ncomponentDidMount\\nIt is called immediately after the component has been inserted into the DOM. That is the ideal place for performing requests to an API, setting subscriptions up or initializing third-party libraries.\\n\\ncomponentDidUpdate\\nIt is called after every update of the component (a change of the props or the state). It lets you respond to the changes — updating the data when an ID changes, for example.\\n\\ncomponentWillUnmount\\nIt is called before the component is removed from the DOM. It is used for cancelling subscriptions, clearing timers or removing event handlers, in order to avoid memory leaks.\\n\\ncomponentDidCatch\\nIt catches the errors that have arisen in the child components (in the render or lifecycle methods or in the constructors). It lets you display a fallback UI and log the error.\\n\\nConclusion: these methods are necessary for managing side effects and resources in class components. With the appearance of hooks in functional components they have been replaced by useEffect and Error Boundary, but an understanding of the lifecycle methods matters for working with legacy code and for a deep understanding of React.","class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    fetch('/api/data')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => this.setState({ data }));\\n  }\\n  render() { return <div>{this.state?.data}</div>; }\\n}\\n\\ncomponentDidUpdate(prevProps) {\\n  if (this.props.userId !== prevProps.userId) {\\n    this.fetchUser(this.props.userId);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ncomponentWillUnmount() {\\n  clearInterval(this.timer);\\n  this.subscription.unsubscribe();\\n}\\n\\ncomponentDidCatch(error, info) {\\n  console.error('Error caught:', error, info);\\n  this.setState({ hasError: true });\\n}\\nrender() {\\n  if (this.state.hasError) return <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;\\n  return this.props.children;\\n}"],[2573,"useEffect lets you perform side effects in functional components. It replaces the lifecycle methods componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate and componentWillUnmount. You pass in a function and an array of dependencies; the effect is started after the render and when the dependencies change. The function returned is used for the clean-up (unsubscribing from events, for example).","What useEffect is, and what it is for\\nuseEffect is a hook in React, which lets you perform side effects in functional components. Side effects are the operations that affect the outside world or depend on it — requests to an API, subscriptions to events, work with timers or the changing of the DOM by hand, for example. In class components the lifecycle methods such as componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate and componentWillUnmount were used for that. useEffect unites them in one API, making the code cleaner and more predictable.\\n\\nHow does useEffect work?\\nThe hook takes two arguments: the effect function and an optional array of dependencies. The effect function runs after every render of the component, if the dependencies stated have changed. If the array of dependencies is empty, the effect runs only once, after the mounting. If no dependencies are stated, the effect is started after every render. The function can return a clean-up function, which is called before the component unmounts or before the effect is started again.\\n\\nExamples of the usage\\nLet us look at a simple example: the loading of data from a server when a component mounts.\\n\\nIn this example the useEffect performs a request to the API when it mounts and every time userId changes. If we had not stated the dependency, the request would be performed on every render, which is inefficient.\\n\\nAnother example is a subscription to an event and its clean-up:\\n\\nThe clean-up function removes the event handler, preventing memory leaks.\\n\\nConclusion\\nuseEffect is a powerful tool for managing side effects in functional React components. It makes the code more declarative and reduces the duplication, replacing several lifecycle methods with one hook. Use it for every operation that is not related directly to the rendering, and always state the right dependencies, in order to avoid unexpected behaviour.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`)\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(data => setUser(data));\\n  }, [userId]); // the effect is started when userId changes\\n\\n  return <div>{user ? user.name : 'Loading...'}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction WindowSizeTracker() {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const handleResize = () => console.log(window.innerWidth);\\n    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // an empty array — the effect only on the mounting\\n\\n  return <div>Resize the window</div>;\\n}"],[2574,"Recursive components call themselves in order to handle nested data. The component takes an array of items and for every item renders its content and then calls itself recursively for the child items. It matters to pass a unique key to every item and to handle the base case properly, when there are no child items.","What are recursive components?\\nRecursive components are components that call themselves in order to handle nested structures of data. That is especially useful when working with trees, multi-level menus, comments with replies or file systems.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nThe component takes an array of data and for every item renders its content. If an item has child items, the component calls itself recursively in order to display them. The base case is the absence of child items, which prevents infinite recursion.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe important points\\n\\n- Always pass a unique key to every item, for an efficient update of the DOM.\\n- Check for the presence of child items before the recursive call, in order to avoid errors.\\n- Avoid deep nesting, since it can lead to performance problems.\\n\\nConclusion\\nRecursive components are a powerful tool for working with nested data. They are worth applying when the structure of the data has an indeterminate depth — in tree menus, file systems or multi-level comments, for example.","function TreeNode({ node }) {\\n  return (\\n    <li>\\n      {node.name}\\n      {node.children && (\\n        <ul>\\n          {node.children.map(child => (\\n            <TreeNode key={child.id} node={child} />\\n          ))}\\n        </ul>\\n      )}\\n    </li>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Tree({ data }) {\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {data.map(node => (\\n        <TreeNode key={node.id} node={node} />\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2575,"A controlled form in React is managed through the component's state. For every input field a separate value is created in the state, and an onChange handler updates the corresponding field. That lets React control the fields' values and makes the validation and the submission of the data easier.","What is a controlled form?\\nIn React a controlled form is a form whose fields' values are held in the component's state and updated through event handlers. React becomes the single source of truth for the form's data, which makes the management, the validation and the submission easier.\\n\\nThe implementation with several fields\\nFor several fields it is convenient to use an object in the state, in which the keys correspond to the fields' names. The onChange handler uses the field's name attribute in order to update the state dynamically.\\n\\nThe advantages and the application\\n\\n- Simple validation: you can check the data on every change.\\n- It is easy to send the data to a server: the state already contains all of the values.\\n- It is convenient for dynamic forms, where the number of fields can change.\\n\\nConclusion: controlled forms with several input fields are applied in React for creating interactive, manageable user interfaces, especially when validation or complex logic for submitting the data is required.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({\\n    username: '',\\n    email: '',\\n    password: ''\\n  });\\n\\n  const handleChange = (e) => {\\n    const { name, value } = e.target;\\n    setFormData(prev => ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));\\n  };\\n\\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n    e.preventDefault();\\n    console.log(formData);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n      <input name=\\"username\\" value={formData.username} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      <input name=\\"email\\" value={formData.email} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      <input name=\\"password\\" type=\\"password\\" value={formData.password} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2576,"Moving a handler function outside a component is justified when it does not depend on the component's props or state and uses no closures over its internal variables. That lets you avoid creating a new function on every render, which improves the performance. Static event handlers, such as click handlers that need no access to the state, can be moved outside, for example. If the function depends on the state or the props, however, it is better to use useCallback.","Why move a handler function outside a component?\\nIn React, on every render of a component all of the functions declared inside it are created afresh. That can lead to redundant re-renders of the child components, if they receive those functions as props. Moving a function outside the component lets you preserve the reference to one and the same function between renders, which optimizes the performance.\\n\\nWhen is that justified?\\n\\n- The function does not use the component's state or props (it is pure and static).\\n- The function does not depend on closures over the component's internal variables.\\n- The component re-renders often, and the child components use React.memo or PureComponent.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen should you not move it out?\\nIf the function depends on the state or the props, moving it out will lead to the loss of access to the current values. In such cases use a useCallback with the right dependencies.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMoving handler functions outside a component is a simple, effective way of optimizing, which is worth applying for static handlers that do not depend on the component's internal state.","// The unoptimized version: the function is created every time\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    console.log('Clicked');\\n  };\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>;\\n}\\n\\n// The optimized version: the function has been moved outside\\nconst handleClick = () => {\\n  console.log('Clicked');\\n};\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>;\\n}"],[2577,"The onChange event fires every time the user changes an input field's value — types or deletes a character, for example. In React it is used for updating the component's state on every change, which lets you create controlled components. The handler receives an event object, from which the current value can be obtained through event.target.value.","How the onChange event works in a form's fields\\nThe \`onChange\` event in HTML and JavaScript fires when the user changes the content of a form element such as an \`<input>\`, a \`<select>\` or a \`<textarea>\`. Unlike the \`oninput\` event, which fires on every change, \`onchange\` normally fires once the focus is lost, but in React it behaves like \`oninput\` and is called on every change of the value.\\n\\nThe use in React\\nIn React the \`onChange\` event is used for creating controlled components, where the form's state is held in the component and updated through a handler. That lets React control the field's value and respond to every change.\\n\\nIn this example, on every character typed \`handleChange\` is called, which updates the \`name\` state. The input field displays the current value from the state, which makes it controlled.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe \`onChange\` event is widely used in forms for real-time validation, autocompletion, a search with suggestions and other interactive elements. It lets you respond to every change of the input, ensuring the interface updates dynamically.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`onChange\` in React for creating controlled components, when the state of a form's fields has to be tracked and managed in real time. That is especially useful for validation, filtering and other scenarios that require an immediate reaction to the user's input.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const [name, setName] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleChange = (event) => {\\n    setName(event.target.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <input type=\\"text\\" value={name} onChange={handleChange} />\\n  );\\n}"],[2578,"Rather than several useState calls for each of a form's fields, you can use a single state object. For that, one useState hook is created with an object containing all of the fields. The update happens through the setState function, using the spread operator or a functional update, so as to keep the remaining fields unchanged. That makes the code simpler and the management of the form more centralized.","Uniting a form's fields in one state object\\nIn React, in order to manage a form, several useState hooks are often used for each field. That leads to duplicated code and complicates the maintenance, however. A more effective approach is to hold all of the fields in one state object. That lets you manage the form centrally and makes the resetting, the validation and the submission of the data easier.\\n\\nHow this works\\nRather than creating separate pieces of state for every field, you create one object in which the keys are the fields' names and the values are their current values. For the update, the setState function is used, which takes a new object or a function returning a new object on the basis of the previous state. It matters to use the spread operator (...prevState) or Object.assign, so as not to lose the other fields.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe advantages and the application\\n\\n- It makes the code simpler: fewer hooks, easier to read and to maintain.\\n- It is convenient for resetting the form: it is enough to call setFormData with the initial object.\\n- It is easy to pass the data to an API or for validation.\\n- It suits forms of any size, especially ones with a large number of fields.\\n\\nConclusion: use a single state object for a form when centralized management, simpler code and convenience in working with the data are needed. This approach is especially useful in complex forms with a great many fields.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({\\n    username: '',\\n    email: '',\\n    password: ''\\n  });\\n\\n  const handleChange = (e) => {\\n    const { name, value } = e.target;\\n    setFormData(prevState => ({\\n      ...prevState,\\n      [name]: value\\n    }));\\n  };\\n\\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n    e.preventDefault();\\n    console.log(formData);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n      <input name=\\"username\\" value={formData.username} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      <input name=\\"email\\" value={formData.email} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      <input name=\\"password\\" type=\\"password\\" value={formData.password} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2579,"Virtual lists with elements of a variable height require the dynamic measurement of every element's height. The approach normally used is a precomputation of the positions on the basis of known sizes, or the use of libraries that measure the height after the rendering. That lets you compute the visible area properly and position the elements, avoiding the complete drawing of the whole list.","The basic concept\\nVirtual lists (or the virtualization of lists) are a technique in which only a small part of the elements is displayed, the part visible in the viewport, while the remaining elements are replaced with empty placeholders. When the elements have a variable height, the standard approaches with a fixed height do not work, since it is impossible to determine every element's exact position in advance.\\n\\nThe methods of handling a variable height\\n\\n- Pre-measurement: all of the elements are rendered once (possibly off-screen), their height is measured and saved in an array. The list then uses that data for computing the positions.\\n- Dynamic measurement: the height is measured at the moment the element first appears in the visible area. After the measurement, the positions of the following elements are corrected.\\n- An estimate on the basis of the average: the average height of an element is used for an approximate computation, which can lead to errors in the scrolling but is simpler to implement.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation in JavaScript\\n\\nThe application in practice\\nLibraries such as react-window or react-virtualized provide components for working with a variable height. In react-window, for example, the VariableSizeList component is used, which takes a function for obtaining every element's height.\\n\\nConclusion\\nVirtual lists with a variable height are necessary for working efficiently with dynamic data, where the size of the elements is unknown in advance. They are widely applied in chats, news feeds and tables with changeable content, ensuring smooth scrolling and low memory consumption.","class VirtualList {\\n  constructor(container, items, estimatedHeight = 50) {\\n    this.container = container;\\n    this.items = items;\\n    this.estimatedHeight = estimatedHeight;\\n    this.heights = new Array(items.length).fill(estimatedHeight);\\n    this.positions = [];\\n    this.calculatePositions();\\n  }\\n\\n  calculatePositions() {\\n    let offset = 0;\\n    this.positions = this.heights.map((h) => {\\n      const pos = offset;\\n      offset += h;\\n      return pos;\\n    });\\n  }\\n\\n  updateHeight(index, actualHeight) {\\n    this.heights[index] = actualHeight;\\n    this.calculatePositions();\\n    this.render();\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    const scrollTop = this.container.scrollTop;\\n    const containerHeight = this.container.clientHeight;\\n    const startIndex = this.findIndex(scrollTop);\\n    const endIndex = this.findIndex(scrollTop + containerHeight);\\n    // the drawing of the elements from startIndex to endIndex\\n  }\\n\\n  findIndex(offset) {\\n    // a binary search over this.positions\\n  }\\n}"],[2580,"Yes, in React arbitrary data unrelated to the DOM can be held in refs. useRef creates an object with a current property, which is preserved between renders. A change of current does not cause a re-render of the component. That is convenient for holding timers, previous values, references to instances of classes or any other mutable data that should not affect the display.","What useRef is, and how it works\\nuseRef is a React hook, which returns a mutable object with a single current property. Unlike useState, a change of the value of current does not cause a repeat render of the component. That makes useRef ideal for holding data that has to be preserved between renders but does not affect the UI.\\n\\nHolding arbitrary data\\nBesides references to DOM elements, useRef can hold any data: numbers, strings, objects, functions, instances of classes. You can hold a timer's identifier, a prop's previous value or a reference to an external service, for example.\\n\\nWhen is this useful\\n\\n- Holding timers and intervals for their subsequent clean-up\\n- Preserving previous values of props or state for a comparison\\n- Holding references to external libraries or instances of classes\\n- Caching the results of computations without redundant renders\\n\\nConclusion\\nuseRef is a powerful tool for holding mutable data that should not affect the render. Use it for timers, previous values and any data that has to be preserved between renders without causing an update of the component.","import { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Timer() {\\n  const timerRef = useRef(null);\\n  const countRef = useRef(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    timerRef.current = setInterval(() => {\\n      countRef.current += 1;\\n      console.log('Count:', countRef.current);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    return () => clearInterval(timerRef.current);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <div>Check console</div>;\\n}"],[2581,"To send a GET request through HttpClient, you have to import HttpClientModule in the AppModule and then inject HttpClient into a service or a component. Call the method http.get(url), which returns an Observable. Subscribe to it by means of subscribe() in order to obtain the data, or use the async pipe in the template.","The basics of working with HttpClient in Angular\\nHttpClient is Angular's built-in service for performing HTTP requests. It is built on the basis of RxJS Observables, which lets you work with asynchronous data conveniently and apply operators for transforming it.\\n\\nThe configuration and the import\\nFirst you have to import HttpClientModule in the application's root module (normally the AppModule). That is done in the imports section.\\n\\nSending a GET request\\nOnce it is configured, you can inject HttpClient into any service or component through the constructor. To send a GET request, the get() method is used, which takes a URL and optional parameters.\\n\\nHandling the response\\nThe get() method returns an Observable. To obtain the data, you have to subscribe to it. In a component that can be done like this:\\n\\nYou can also use the async pipe in the template, for the automatic subscription and unsubscription.\\n\\nConclusion\\nHttpClient makes working with HTTP in Angular easier, providing typed responses and integration with RxJS. It is worth applying for every network request in Angular applications, since it provides convenient handling of errors, interceptors and testing.","import { HttpClientModule } from '@angular/common/http';\\n\\n@NgModule({\\n  imports: [\\n    HttpClientModule\\n  ]\\n})\\nexport class AppModule { }\\n\\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\\nimport { Injectable } from '@angular/core';\\n\\n@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })\\nexport class DataService {\\n  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}\\n\\n  getUsers() {\\n    return this.http.get('https://api.example.com/users');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nthis.dataService.getUsers().subscribe({\\n  next: (data) => console.log(data),\\n  error: (err) => console.error(err)\\n});"],[2582,"A BehaviorSubject is a variety of Subject in RxJS, which holds the last value and passes it to new subscribers. Unlike an ordinary Subject, a BehaviorSubject requires an initial value when it is created. On a subscription a BehaviorSubject emits the current value at once, while a Subject emits only new events. That makes a BehaviorSubject convenient for holding state — the current user or the design theme, for example.","What is a BehaviorSubject?\\nA BehaviorSubject is a special type of Subject from the RxJS library, which is an observable capable of holding a current value. When a BehaviorSubject is created, an initial value must be passed in. When a new subscriber subscribes to a BehaviorSubject, it receives the last (or the initial) value immediately and then keeps receiving all of the subsequent updates.\\n\\nThe differences from an ordinary Subject\\nAn ordinary Subject holds no value. It simply passes events to all of the current subscribers. If a subscriber subscribes after an event has already happened, it will not receive it. A BehaviorSubject, by contrast, always holds the last value and emits it to new subscribers.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nA BehaviorSubject is often used for holding global state in applications — the current user, the settings or the theme, for example. It is convenient when a new component has to receive the current state at once, without additional requests.\\n\\nConclusion: a BehaviorSubject should be applied when the current state has to be held and passed to new subscribers, unlike a Subject, which suits only the broadcasting of events without the preservation of any history.","import { Subject, BehaviorSubject } from 'rxjs';\\n\\nconst subject = new Subject();\\nconst behaviorSubject = new BehaviorSubject('initial');\\n\\nsubject.subscribe(value => console.log('Subject:', value));\\nbehaviorSubject.subscribe(value => console.log('BehaviorSubject:', value));\\n\\nsubject.next('A');\\nbehaviorSubject.next('A');\\n\\n// A new subscriber after the events\\nsubject.subscribe(value => console.log('Subject late:', value));\\nbehaviorSubject.subscribe(value => console.log('BehaviorSubject late:', value));\\n\\n// The output:\\n// Subject: A\\n// BehaviorSubject: initial\\n// BehaviorSubject: A\\n// BehaviorSubject late: A\\n// Subject late: (nothing)"],[2583,"To obtain the value from an input event, use event.target.value. When the user types text in a field, the browser creates an event object. The target property points to the element that caused the event, while value contains that element's current text value. That is the standard way of accessing a form's data in React and in native JavaScript.","How event.target.value works\\nWhen the user interacts with a form element (types text in an \`<input>\`, for example), the browser generates an event. The event object contains information about the action that has happened. The \`target\` property refers to the DOM element that initiated the event. That element has a \`value\` property, which holds the input field's current content.\\n\\nAn example of the usage in native JavaScript\\n\\nIn this example, on every character typed in the field with the id 'myInput', a function is called, which obtains the field's value through \`event.target.value\` and prints it to the console.\\n\\nThe use in React\\nIn React the handling of events is similar, but the JSX syntax is used. The value is normally saved in the component's state:\\n\\nHere \`event.target.value\` obtains the field's new value, which then updates the component's state through \`setText\`. That lets React control the field's value and respond to the changes.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Feedback, registration and login forms\\n- Search boxes with autocompletion\\n- Text editors and data-entry fields\\n\\nConclusion: \`event.target.value\` is the main way of obtaining data from input fields in web development. It is used both in native JavaScript and in the modern frameworks (React, Vue, Angular), for handling the user's input and managing a form's state.","document.getElementById('myInput').addEventListener('input', function(event) {\\n  const inputValue = event.target.value;\\n  console.log('The current value:', inputValue);\\n});\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const [text, setText] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleChange = (event) => {\\n    setText(event.target.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return <input type=\\"text\\" value={text} onChange={handleChange} />;\\n}"],[2584,"A useEffect guarantees the request is performed after the render rather than during it, which prevents the UI being blocked and ensures the synchronization with the current state. The onChange handler is called synchronously on every change, which can lead to a great many unnecessary requests and to performance problems. A useEffect also lets you manage the cancellation of the requests and the dependencies easily.","Why a useEffect rather than onChange?\\nIn React event handlers such as onChange run synchronously during the render. If you place a fetch in them, a new request will be started every time the input field changes, which can lead to an avalanche of requests and to worse performance. Besides, the component's state can change before the request completes, which will lead to a race in the data.\\n\\nThe role of useEffect\\nThe useEffect hook is intended for performing side effects after the component has rendered. That guarantees the data requests do not block the render and are performed only when the dependencies stated change. useEffect also provides a clean-up mechanism, which lets you cancel the previous requests if a dependency has changed before they complete.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing a useEffect for requests when the state changes ensures predictable behaviour, prevents races in the data and lets you manage the cancellation of the requests efficiently. That is especially useful in scenarios of a search with autocompletion or of filtering, where the performance and the currency of the data matter.","import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction SearchComponent() {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\\n  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    if (!query) return;\\n    const controller = new AbortController();\\n    const fetchData = async () => {\\n      try {\\n        const res = await fetch(\`/api/search?q=\${query}\`, {\\n          signal: controller.signal\\n        });\\n        const data = await res.json();\\n        setResults(data);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        if (err.name !== 'AbortError') console.error(err);\\n      }\\n    };\\n    fetchData();\\n    return () => controller.abort(); // the cancellation of the previous request\\n  }, [query]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />\\n  );\\n}"],[2585,"Effector is a reactive state manager based on events and streams, unlike Redux, which uses actions and reducers. In Effector there is no single store; independent stores are used instead, which are updated through events. The main differences: built-in support for asynchrony through effects, the absence of boilerplate, and simpler work with the dependencies.","Effector's basic concept\\nEffector is a modern state manager for JavaScript applications, which uses a reactive approach. Unlike Redux, where the state is managed through actions and reducers, Effector relies on events and stores. Events are functions that can change the stores' state, while stores are reactive containers of data, which notify the subscribers of the changes automatically.\\n\\nThe key differences from Redux\\n\\n- The architecture: in Redux there is a single global store, while in Effector you can create a great many independent stores, which makes the modularity easier.\\n- Asynchrony: Effector provides built-in effects for working with asynchronous operations such as requests to an API, without the need to use middleware (Redux Thunk, for example).\\n- Boilerplate: in Redux a lot of boilerplate code is required (action types, action creators, reducers); in Effector the code is more concise.\\n- Reactivity: Effector uses reactive links between the stores and the events, which lets it update the data automatically when the dependencies change.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nEffector suits complex applications with a large number of interrelated pieces of state well, where the performance and the predictability matter. It is often used in React projects, where flexible state management without excessive boilerplate is required.\\n\\nConclusion: Effector is worth applying when fast, reactive state management with minimal boilerplate code is needed, especially in projects with frequent asynchronous operations and complex dependencies between pieces of data.","import { createStore, createEvent, createEffect } from 'effector';\\n\\n// We create an event\\nconst increment = createEvent();\\n\\n// We create a store with an initial value\\nconst counter = createStore(0)\\n  .on(increment, (state) => state + 1);\\n\\n// We create an effect for an asynchronous request\\nconst fetchUser = createEffect(async (id) => {\\n  const response = await fetch(\`/api/users/\${id}\`);\\n  return response.json();\\n});\\n\\n// We subscribe to the store's changes\\ncounter.watch((value) => console.log('Counter:', value));\\n\\n// We call the event\\nincrement(); // Counter: 1"],[2586,"Yup is a library for validating data on the basis of schemas. It lets you describe the rules for checking objects, strings, numbers and other types. Most often it is used with forms in React, for checking the user's input before the submission.","The purpose of Yup\\nYup is a library for creating and running schemas for validating data in JavaScript. It provides a declarative API for describing the rules for checking values such as strings, numbers, arrays and objects. Yup's main aim is to make the validation of data easier, especially in forms, by making the code more readable and more maintainable.\\n\\nHow this works\\nYou define a schema, which describes the expected structure of the data and the rules for every field. Then you call the \`validate\` or \`isValid\` method, in order to check whether the object matches the schema. If the data does not pass the check, Yup returns comprehensible error messages.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nYup is often used together with form-management libraries such as Formik or React Hook Form. That lets you describe the validation rules centrally and display the errors next to the input fields automatically.\\n\\nConclusion\\nYup is useful for creating reliable, comprehensible validation of data in web applications, especially when working with forms. It helps you avoid duplicating code and makes the handling of the errors easier.","import * as yup from 'yup';\\n\\nconst schema = yup.object().shape({\\n  name: yup.string().required('The name is required'),\\n  age: yup.number().positive().integer(),\\n  email: yup.string().email('An incorrect email'),\\n});\\n\\nschema.validate({ name: 'Anna', age: 25, email: 'anna@test.com' })\\n  .then(() => console.log('The validation has passed'))\\n  .catch(err => console.log(err.errors));"],[2587,"React Query caches the results of the requests automatically, using unique query keys. The data counts as fresh for the staleTime set, after which it is marked as stale and can be requested again when the component mounts again, or on a timer. The cache is held in memory and can be configured by means of the gcTime option (formerly cacheTime), for managing how long unused data is held.","The basics of caching in React Query\\nReact Query (TanStack Query) provides a powerful mechanism for caching the data obtained from a server. Every request is identified by a unique query key, which is used for holding the data in the cache and retrieving it. The cache is held in the application's memory and is managed automatically.\\n\\nThe key concepts\\n\\n- staleTime — the time in milliseconds during which the data counts as fresh. By default 0, which means the data is marked as stale at once.\\n- gcTime (formerly cacheTime) — the time during which unused data stays in the cache before being removed. By default 5 minutes.\\n- refetchOnMount — an automatic repeat request when the component mounts, if the data has gone stale.\\n- refetchOnWindowFocus — a repeat request when the browser's window gains focus.\\n\\nAn example of configuring the caching\\n\\nThe invalidation of the cache\\nTo update the data after a mutation, the invalidation of the cache is used, by means of queryClient.invalidateQueries. That marks the data as stale and initiates a repeat request.\\n\\nConclusion\\nCaching in React Query lets you cut the number of requests to the server down considerably, improve the performance and ensure the data is current. It is recommended that you use staleTime for data that changes rarely, and gcTime for managing the memory.","import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';\\n\\nfunction useTodos() {\\n  return useQuery({\\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\\n    queryFn: fetchTodos,\\n    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // the data counts as fresh for 5 minutes\\n    gcTime: 10 * 60 * 1000,   // the data is held in the cache for 10 minutes after the component is removed\\n  });\\n}\\n\\nimport { useMutation, useQueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query';\\n\\nfunction useAddTodo() {\\n  const queryClient = useQueryClient();\\n  return useMutation({\\n    mutationFn: addTodo,\\n    onSuccess: () => {\\n      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] });\\n    },\\n  });\\n}"],[2588,"If key is not stated when rendering a list in React, the warning will appear: \\"Warning: Each child in a list should have a unique 'key' prop.\\" That warning indicates that React cannot track the changes of the list's elements efficiently. Without a key React will repaint the whole list on every update, which reduces the performance.","What is key in React?\\nThe \`key\` attribute is a special string attribute, which helps React identify every element in a list. When you render an array of data by means of \`map()\`, React uses the \`key\` in order to match the elements before and after an update. That is a key part of the reconciliation process, which lets React minimize the changes in the real DOM.\\n\\nWhy does the warning arise?\\nIf \`key\` is not stated, React prints a warning to the browser's console: \`Warning: Each child in a list should have a unique 'key' prop.\` That is not an error but a recommendation. Without a \`key\` React uses the element's index in the array as the implicit key, which can lead to the components' state being updated wrongly, especially when elements are added, deleted or rearranged.\\n\\nAn example of the code with the warning\\n\\nHow to use key properly\\nUse a unique identifier from the data — an \`id\`, for example:\\n\\nIf there is no unique id, you can use the index, but that is a last resort, since the index is unstable when the list changes.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAlways state a \`key\` when rendering lists in React, using stable unique identifiers. That improves the performance and prevents errors in the updating of the state, especially in dynamic lists with the addition or the removal of elements.","function TodoList() {\\n  const items = ['Buy some milk', 'Call a friend', 'Do the homework'];\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {items.map((item) => (\\n        <li>{item}</li>  // the key is not stated — there will be a warning\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction TodoList() {\\n  const items = [\\n    { id: 1, text: 'Buy some milk' },\\n    { id: 2, text: 'Call a friend' },\\n  ];\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {items.map((item) => (\\n        <li key={item.id}>{item.text}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2589,"React Hook Form uses uncontrolled components and minimizes the number of re-renders, which makes it more performant. Final Form is based on controlled components and provides a more flexible architecture with subscriptions to the changes. React Hook Form is simpler to integrate with TypeScript and has a smaller bundle size.","The main differences between React Hook Form and Final Form\\nReact Hook Form and Final Form are two popular libraries for managing forms in React, but they have fundamentally different approaches to the work. React Hook Form is built on uncontrolled components, which lets it avoid redundant re-renders and improve the performance. Final Form, on the contrary, uses controlled components and provides more detailed control over the form's state through subscriptions.\\n\\nThe architecture and the performance\\nReact Hook Form minimizes the number of re-renders, since it does not hold the form's state in React state but uses refs for access to the fields' values. That is especially useful for large forms with a great many fields. Final Form uses subscriptions to the changes, which lets it update only the components that are subscribed to particular fields, but it can still be less performant on very large forms.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe integration and the ecosystem\\nReact Hook Form has built-in support for TypeScript and integrates easily with UI libraries such as Material-UI or Ant Design. Final Form supports TypeScript too, but it requires more manual configuration for working with external components. React Hook Form has a smaller bundle size (about 8KB), which makes it preferable for projects where the loading performance matters.\\n\\nConclusion: React Hook Form suits projects where the performance and ease of use matter better, especially with TypeScript. Final Form is worth choosing if a more flexible architecture with detailed control over the subscriptions and the form's state is required.","// React Hook Form\\nimport { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const { register, handleSubmit } = useForm();\\n  const onSubmit = (data) => console.log(data);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>\\n      <input {...register('name')} />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Final Form\\nimport { Form, Field } from 'react-final-form';\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const onSubmit = (values) => console.log(values);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <Form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\\n      {({ handleSubmit }) => (\\n        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n          <Field name=\\"name\\">\\n            {({ input }) => <input {...input} />}\\n          </Field>\\n          <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button>\\n        </form>\\n      )}\\n    </Form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2590,"In React access to an uncontrolled component is obtained through refs. Create a ref by means of useRef and attach it to the element through the ref attribute. Then address the current value through ref.current.value. That lets you obtain the data directly from the DOM, bypassing React's state.","What are uncontrolled components?\\nUncontrolled components in React are form elements that hold their state in the DOM rather than in React's state. Unlike controlled components, where every change of a field is synchronized with the state through an onChange, uncontrolled components rely on refs for access to the current values.\\n\\nHow do you obtain access?\\nFor access to an uncontrolled component, the \`useRef\` hook is used. It creates an object with a \`current\` property, which refers to the DOM element. An example:\\n\\nIn this example \`inputRef\` is attached to the input field. When the form is submitted, we obtain the value through \`inputRef.current.value\`.\\n\\nWhen to use it?\\nUncontrolled components are useful for simple forms, where instant validation or complex logic is not required. They are also convenient when integrating with libraries that manage the DOM directly. For complex forms with dynamic changes, however, it is better to use controlled components.\\n\\nConclusion: use uncontrolled components and refs for fast access to a form's data, when complete control over every change is not needed.","import React, { useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n    e.preventDefault();\\n    alert('The value: ' + inputRef.current.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n      <input ref={inputRef} type=\\"text\\" />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Send</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2591,"Controlled components hold their state in React, through useState, and are updated through an onChange. Uncontrolled components use a ref for direct access to the DOM element, while the state is held in the DOM itself. Controlled ones give more control and predictability; uncontrolled ones are simpler for simple forms.","The main difference\\nControlled and uncontrolled components are two approaches to working with forms in React. In controlled components an input field's state is held in the React component, through \`useState\`, and every change updates that state through an \`onChange\` handler. In uncontrolled components the state is held in the DOM itself, and \`useRef\` is used for access to the value.\\n\\nAn example of a controlled component\\n\\nAn example of an uncontrolled component\\n\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- Controlled components: when instant control over the input is needed (validation, masks, dynamic changes).\\n- Uncontrolled components: for simple forms without complex logic, where every change does not have to be tracked.\\n\\nConclusion: controlled components are preferable in most cases, since they make the state predictable and make testing easier. Uncontrolled components can be useful for integration with non-React code or for very simple fields.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction ControlledInput() {\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleChange = (e) => setValue(e.target.value);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <input\\n      type=\\"text\\"\\n      value={value}\\n      onChange={handleChange}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UncontrolledInput() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const handleSubmit = () => {\\n    alert(inputRef.current.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input type=\\"text\\" ref={inputRef} />\\n      <button onClick={handleSubmit}>Submit</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2592,"The shouldComponentUpdate method in class components is analogous in meaning to React.memo. It lets you prevent a repeat rendering of the component if the props have not changed. React.memo does the same for functional components, by wrapping them and performing a shallow comparison of the props.","The relation of shouldComponentUpdate to React.memo\\nIn React class components have the lifecycle method \`shouldComponentUpdate\`, which is called before the rendering when the component receives new props or state. That method returns \`true\` (by default) or \`false\`. If it returns \`false\`, the rendering and the subsequent lifecycle methods are skipped. That lets you optimize the performance by avoiding unnecessary updates.\\n\\n\`React.memo\` is a higher-order function for functional components. It wraps a component and performs a shallow comparison of the previous and the new props. If the props have not changed, the component does not re-render. In essence, \`React.memo\` implements the logic of \`shouldComponentUpdate\` with a shallow comparison automatically.\\n\\nExamples of the code\\nA class component with shouldComponentUpdate:\\n\\nA functional component with React.memo:\\n\\nIf a custom comparison is needed, \`React.memo\` takes a second argument — a comparison function:\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`shouldComponentUpdate\` in class components and \`React.memo\` in functional ones for preventing redundant renders, when the props change rarely. That is especially useful for components that render heavy elements or update often.","class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n  shouldComponentUpdate(nextProps) {\\n    // We compare only the props needed\\n    return nextProps.value !== this.props.value;\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>{this.props.value}</div>;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(({ value }) => {\\n  return <div>{value}</div>\\n});\\n// By default a shallow comparison of the props\\n\\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(\\n  ({ value }) => <div>{value}</div>,\\n  (prevProps, nextProps) => prevProps.value === nextProps.value\\n);"],[2593,"React uses a unidirectional data flow. The data is passed from parent components to child ones through props. For the feedback, child components call callbacks passed in through props. The state is lifted up the hierarchy (lifting state up), so that several components can share it.","The unidirectional data flow in React\\nReact uses a unidirectional data flow. That means the data is passed only from the top down — from parent components to child ones. Such an approach makes debugging easier and makes the application's behaviour predictable.\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe parent component passes the data to the child one through attributes called props. The child component cannot change that data directly. If the state has to be changed, the child component calls a callback function passed in through props, which updates the state in the parent.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nA unidirectional data flow prevents the chaotic changes of state that are hard to track. Every component receives data only from its parent and cannot change it directly. That makes the code more predictable and makes refactoring easier.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe unidirectional data flow in React is a fundamental principle, which ensures the application is stable and predictable. It is worth applying in every React project, for making state management and debugging easier.","// The parent component\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const increment = () => setCount(count + 1);\\n\\n  return <Child count={count} onIncrement={increment} />;\\n}\\n\\n// The child component\\nfunction Child({ count, onIncrement }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={onIncrement}>+1</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2594,"Prop drilling arises when data is passed through several levels of components, even if the intermediate components do not use it. That makes the code cumbersome and hard to maintain. State managers (Redux, Zustand, for example) or the Context API let you hold global state and provide it to the components that need it directly, bypassing the intermediate links.","The problem of prop drilling\\nIn React data is passed from a parent component to the child ones through props. As an application grows, a situation arises in which props have to be passed through several levels of nesting, even though the intermediate components do not use them. That is called prop drilling. Such an approach complicates refactoring, increases the coupling of the components and makes the code less readable.\\n\\nHow state managers solve that problem\\nState managers (Redux, MobX, Zustand, for example) or the built-in Context API provide a mechanism for holding global state, available to any component without an explicit passing through props. A component can subscribe to the part of the state it needs and receive the updates automatically.\\n\\nAn example with the Context API\\n\\nIn this example Toolbar receives no props, while ThemedButton uses the context directly. That eliminates the prop drilling.\\n\\nConclusion\\nState managers and the Context API solve the problem of prop drilling, making the application's architecture simpler and the code more maintainable. They are worth applying when data is used by many components at different levels of nesting, in order to avoid the superfluous passing of props.","import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext();\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  return <ThemedButton />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction ThemedButton() {\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>\\n      Current theme: {theme}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}"],[2595,"A HOC is a function that takes a component and returns a new component with additional properties or logic. In state managers — in Redux, for example — the \`connect\` HOC is used for subscribing a component to the store and passing it part of the state plus the actions. That lets you separate the state-management logic from the presentation, making the code more modular and more reusable.","What a HOC is, and how it works in state managers\\nA Higher-Order Component (HOC) is a pattern in React, which is a function that takes a component and returns a new component with extended functionality. In the context of state managers such as Redux, a HOC is used for binding a component to the application's global state.\\n\\nAn example with Redux's connect\\nThe best-known example is the \`connect\` function from the \`react-redux\` library. It takes two functions, \`mapStateToProps\` and \`mapDispatchToProps\`, and returns a HOC, which wraps your component. Inside, the HOC subscribes to the Redux store and passes the data and the actions needed through props.\\n\\nHow does it work inside?\\nThe HOC created by \`connect\` uses \`React.Context\` internally, or direct access to the store (through \`useStore\` in the modern versions). It tracks the changes in the store and, when the state updates, repaints the wrapped component, passing it new props. That lets the component stay \\"dumb\\" (purely presentational), knowing nothing about the existence of Redux.\\n\\nThe application in other state managers\\nAn analogous approach is used in MobX (through the \`observer\` HOC) or in libraries for working with Context (\`withTheme\`, for example). A HOC lets you encapsulate the logic of subscribing to the state and makes the components more reusable.\\n\\nConclusion: a HOC in state managers is a powerful tool for separating the responsibility between the management of the state and the display of the UI. It is especially useful in large applications, where centralized state and a clean architecture of the components are required.","import { connect } from 'react-redux';\\n\\nconst mapStateToProps = (state) => ({\\n  count: state.counter.value\\n});\\n\\nconst mapDispatchToProps = (dispatch) => ({\\n  increment: () => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })\\n});\\n\\nconst Counter = ({ count, increment }) => (\\n  <div>\\n    <p>{count}</p>\\n    <button onClick={increment}>+</button>\\n  </div>\\n);\\n\\nexport default connect(mapStateToProps, mapDispatchToProps)(Counter);"],[2596,"In React data is passed from the parent to the child through props. To pass data back, the parent passes a callback function as a prop. The child component calls that function with the data needed, and the parent receives it in its handler. That is called lifting state up.","Passing data from a child component to the parent in React\\nIn React data normally moves from the top down — from the parent to the child components, through props. Often, however, a need arises to pass information back — when the user types text in a field inside a child component, for example, and the parent has to learn that value. The mechanism of callbacks is used for that.\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe parent component defines a handler function and passes it to the child component as a prop. The child component calls that function, passing the data into it (the value from an input, for example). The parent receives that data in its function and can update its state or perform other actions.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\n- Forms: when the data from several input fields has to be gathered and passed upwards.\\n- Lists: on a click on a list item, the parent has to be told which item has been selected.\\n- Modal windows: when a modal window closes, the result has to be passed on (a confirmation, for example).\\n\\nConclusion\\nPassing data through callbacks is the standard, simple way of organizing the feedback from a child component to the parent. This approach should be used when the state has to be lifted a level higher, for the shared use of the data by several components.","// The parent component\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [childData, setChildData] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleDataFromChild = (data) => {\\n    setChildData(data);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>The data from the child: {childData}</h1>\\n      <Child onSendData={handleDataFromChild} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The child component\\nfunction Child({ onSendData }) {\\n  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    onSendData(inputValue); // We pass the data to the parent\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input\\n        type=\\"text\\"\\n        value={inputValue}\\n        onChange={(e) => setInputValue(e.target.value)}\\n      />\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>Send to the parent</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2597,"A HOC is a function that takes a component and returns a new component with additional logic. It solves the problem of duplicated code, when several components use the same business logic — a subscription to data or the handling of authorization, for example. A HOC wraps a component, adding props or behaviour to it, which lets you avoid repeating code and improves the maintainability.","What a HOC is, and what problem it solves\\nA Higher-Order Component (HOC) is a pattern in React, which lets you reuse logic between components. If a great many components contain the same business logic — the loading of data from an API, the checking of access rights or a subscription to events, for example — then without a HOC that logic would have to be duplicated in every component. A HOC solves that problem by acting as a wrapper: it takes a component and returns a new component with added properties or behaviour.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nA HOC is a function that takes a component and returns a new component. Inside the HOC you can implement the shared logic and then pass the data into the wrapped component through props. If authorization logic has to be added to several pages, for example, you can create a \`withAuth\` HOC, which checks whether the user is authorized and either renders the component or redirects to the login page.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nA HOC is often used for:\\n- Adding the logic of a subscription to data (with Redux or Context, for example)\\n- Handling errors or loading\\n- Managing access (authorization)\\n- Logging or analytics\\n\\nWith the appearance of React Hooks (\`useEffect\`, \`useContext\`, for example), however, HOCs have become less popular, since hooks let you reuse logic without creating new components. HOCs nevertheless remain useful in legacy code, or when working with libraries that require them.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA HOC is a powerful tool for reusing logic in React, especially when the same behaviour has to be added to several components without duplicating code. It is worth applying in projects where hooks are not used yet, or when integrating with libraries that expect HOC wrappers.","function withAuth(WrappedComponent) {\\n  return function AuthenticatedComponent(props) {\\n    const isAuthenticated = checkAuth(); // the shared logic\\n    if (!isAuthenticated) {\\n      return <Redirect to=\\"/login\\" />;\\n    }\\n    return <WrappedComponent {...props} />;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst ProtectedPage = withAuth(MyPage);"],[2598,"The user or the code calls dispatch(action). Redux passes the action and the current state into the reducer. The reducer returns the new state. The store saves it and notifies the subscribers. React repaints the components connected through connect or useSelector.","The Redux lifecycle\\nRedux is a predictable state container for JavaScript applications. The whole cycle begins with a call of the dispatch function with an action object, which contains a type and optional data. Redux then passes the current state and the action into the root reducer, which decides how to change the state. A reducer has to be a pure function: it does not change the original state but returns a new object. After that the store updates its internal state and calls all of the subscribers registered through subscribe. In a React application the subscription is carried out through react-redux: connect, or the useSelector/useDispatch hooks. When the store notifies of the change, React starts a re-render of the components that depend on the part of the state that has changed.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of this cycle is necessary for debugging, for optimizing the performance and for designing the architecture of Redux applications properly. Use this approach when centralized state management with predictable changes is required.","// an action creator\\nconst increment = () => ({ type: 'INCREMENT' });\\n\\n// a reducer\\nconst counter = (state = 0, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// the store\\nconst store = createStore(counter);\\n\\n// the dispatch\\nstore.dispatch(increment());\\nconsole.log(store.getState()); // 1"],[2599,"In React the useEffect hook with an empty array of dependencies is used for loading data from a server. That is the analogue of the componentDidMount method in class components. The hook runs after the component's first render, which guarantees the DOM elements are present. Such an approach prevents redundant requests on every update of the state.","The loading of data in React\\nIn React the \`useEffect\` hook is used for performing side effects such as the loading of data from a server. That hook is called after the component has been rendered and added to the DOM. That guarantees we have access to the DOM elements and that the request does not block the initial render.\\n\\nThe proper use of useEffect\\nTo load the data only once, when the component mounts, you have to pass an empty array of dependencies as the second argument of the \`useEffect\`. That is equivalent to the \`componentDidMount\` method in class components.\\n\\nThe important aspects\\n\\n- Do not use an asynchronous function directly in a \`useEffect\`; it is better to declare it inside and call it\\n- Always handle the errors and the loading states\\n- To cancel the requests when the component unmounts, use the clean-up function in the \`useEffect\`\\n\\nConclusion: the use of a \`useEffect\` with an empty array of dependencies is the standard, recommended way of loading data when a component mounts in React. That ensures predictable behaviour and prevents memory leaks.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserList() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch('/api/users')\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setUsers(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      })\\n      .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies\\n\\n  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {users.map(user => (\\n        <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2600,"React Router v6 introduced several considerable changes. The Switch component has been replaced with Routes, and Route now uses element instead of component. The order of the routes no longer matters, since v6 uses a ranking algorithm. The useHistory hook has been replaced with useNavigate. Support for nested routes and relative links has also appeared in v6.","The main differences between React Router v5 and v6\\nReact Router v6 is a considerable update, which makes the API simpler and the routing more intuitive. The main change is the replacement of the Switch component with Routes, which chooses the most suitable route automatically rather than the first one that matches.\\n\\nThe changes in the route components\\nIn v5 the Switch component was used for grouping Routes, and the order of the routes mattered. In v6 Switch has been replaced with Routes, and Route now takes an element prop instead of component or render. That lets you pass the components in directly:\\n\\nThe navigation and the hooks\\nIn v5 the useHistory hook was used for programmatic navigation. In v6 it has been replaced with useNavigate, which provides a more flexible API:\\n\\nNested routes and relative links\\nReact Router v6 has improved the support for nested routes considerably. Now you can define child routes directly inside the parent component, using an Outlet:\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact Router v6 offers a cleaner, more predictable API, makes working with nested routes easier and improves the performance. When migrating from v5 to v6, it is worth paying attention to the replacement of Switch with Routes, the updating of the navigation hooks, and the refactoring of the nested routes using an Outlet.","// React Router v5\\n<Switch>\\n  <Route path=\\"/home\\" component={Home} />\\n  <Route path=\\"/about\\" component={About} />\\n</Switch>\\n\\n// React Router v6\\n<Routes>\\n  <Route path=\\"/home\\" element={<Home />} />\\n  <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n</Routes>\\n\\n// React Router v5\\nconst history = useHistory();\\nhistory.push('/home');\\n\\n// React Router v6\\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\\nnavigate('/home');\\nnavigate(-1); // for going back\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Routes>\\n      <Route path=\\"/dashboard\\" element={<Dashboard />}>\\n        <Route path=\\"stats\\" element={<Stats />} />\\n        <Route path=\\"settings\\" element={<Settings />} />\\n      </Route>\\n    </Routes>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Dashboard() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Dashboard</h1>\\n      <Outlet /> {/* The child routes will be displayed here */}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"]]`);export{e as default};